    .synopsis = gpresult [/s COMPUTER [/u DOMAIN\USER] [/p PASSWORD]] [/user [DOMAIN\]USER] /r [/scope:user|computer] | /h FILE [/f]
    .option-r = the summary.
    .option-s = the remote computer.
    .option-user = whose data (by default, yours).
    .option-scope-user-computer = only one part.
    .option-h = an HTML report in a file.
    .option-f = overwrite the file.
    .example-1 = what applies here, and what was filtered out
    .example-2 = the computer only
    .example-3 = what a librarian gets on her workstation
man-cryptsetup = sets up encrypted devices with dm-crypt, LUKS2 by default: the whole device (a disk or a partition) is encrypted, behind a header that holds up to 32 key slots. Each slot opens it with its own secret: a passphrase or a key file. luksFormat DEVICE [KEYFILE] writes a new header: everything the device held is lost, and cryptsetup asks you to type YES, in capitals, then a passphrase twice (unless a key file is given). open DEVICE NAME (or luksOpen) asks for a key and creates the clear device /dev/mapper/NAME: the file system goes on that one (mkfs.ext4), never on the encrypted device. close NAME (luksClose) removes it, once nothing is mounted on it. status NAME describes an open mapping; luksDump DEVICE shows the header and its slots; luksAddKey DEVICE [NEWKEYFILE] adds a key (an existing one is asked first); luksRemoveKey DEVICE removes the slot of a passphrase, luksKillSlot DEVICE N a slot by its number; luksUUID DEVICE gives the UUID to write in /etc/crypttab; isLuks DEVICE answers by its exit code; luksHeaderBackup DEVICE --header-backup-file FILE saves the header, without which no key opens anything. Exit codes: 1 wrong parameters, 2 no permission (bad passphrase), 4 wrong device, 5 device already exists or is busy. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = cryptsetup [OPTIONS] luksFormat DEVICE [KEYFILE] ; cryptsetup open DEVICE NAME [--key-file FILE] ; cryptsetup close NAME ; cryptsetup status NAME ; cryptsetup luksDump DEVICE ; cryptsetup luksAddKey DEVICE [NEWKEYFILE] ; cryptsetup luksRemoveKey DEVICE ; cryptsetup luksUUID DEVICE
    .example-1 = find the device: its type, its UUID, whether it is mounted
    .example-2 = encrypt a partition (type YES, then the passphrase twice)
    .example-3 = open it: /dev/mapper/offsite appears
    .example-4 = make the file system on the open mapping
    .example-5 = add a key file in another slot
    .example-6 = the header and its key slots
    .example-7 = describe the open mapping
    .example-8 = close it (unmount first)
    .example-9 = save the header, somewhere else than the disk
man-crypttab = /etc/crypttab describes the encrypted devices to open, one line each: the mapping's name (it becomes /dev/mapper/NAME), the device (preferably UUID=… of the LUKS header: cryptsetup luksUUID or blkid), the key file (none, or -, to ask for a passphrase) and options separated by commas: luks, noauto (not opened at boot, only on demand: cryptdisks_start NAME), nofail (the boot doesn't wait if the device is missing), discard… A removable disk takes noauto or nofail, like its line in /etc/fstab, which mounts /dev/mapper/NAME. The key file must belong to root and be readable by nobody else.
    .synopsis = NAME  DEVICE  KEYFILE  OPTIONS
    .example-1 = read it
    .example-2 = the UUID to write in the second column
    .example-3 = open a device it describes (on Debian)
man-cryptdisks_start = opens an encrypted device described in /etc/crypttab, under its name, with its key file (or by asking for its passphrase if the key file is none), the way the boot would; it warns if the key file can be read by someone other than root. Debian's script, from the cryptsetup package. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = cryptdisks_start NAME…
    .example-1 = open the device named offsite in /etc/crypttab
    .example-2 = then mount it, as /etc/fstab describes it
man-cryptdisks_stop = closes an encrypted device described in /etc/crypttab (like cryptsetup close), unless something is still mounted on it. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = cryptdisks_stop NAME…
    .example-1 = unmount first
    .example-2 = then close it
man-mkfs_ext4 = makes a new ext4 file system on a device (a partition, a disk, an open LUKS mapping /dev/mapper/NAME). Everything the device held is lost: mkfs.ext4 refuses a mounted device, and asks before writing over another file system (-F not to ask). On an encrypted disk, it goes on the open mapping, never on the encrypted partition itself (its LUKS header would be wiped). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = mkfs.ext4 [-L LABEL] [-F] [-q] DEVICE
    .option-L = the file system's label (LABEL=, in /etc/fstab).
    .option-F = don't ask before writing over an existing file system.
    .option-q = quiet: no report.
    .example-1 = the file system inside an encrypted disk
    .example-2 = a file system with a label
man-sha256sum = computes the SHA-256 fingerprint of files: 64 hexadecimal characters that change completely if a single byte changes. sha256sum FILE… writes one line per file ("FINGERPRINT  NAME"), to keep in a file; -c FILE rereads such a list and checks each file again: NAME: OK, or FAILED (and a warning at the end, exit code 1). The names in the list are read from where you are: run it from the same directory. To prove that a restore gives back exactly what was backed up.
    .synopsis = sha256sum FILE… ; sha256sum -c LIST
    .option-c = check the files of a list (--check); --quiet only shows failures, --status shows nothing (only the exit code).
    .example-1 = a file's fingerprint
    .example-2 = keep the fingerprints of several files
    .example-3 = check them, from the same directory
man-export = (built into bash) marks variables to be passed to the programs the shell starts: their environment. export NAME=value sets it and exports it; export alone lists the exported variables. NAME=value written before a command passes it to that command only. sudo starts its command with a clean environment (env_reset): write the variables after sudo (sudo NAME=value command), or use sudo -E, which keeps them. $NAME is replaced by its value on the command line.
    .synopsis = export [NAME[=VALUE]…] ; NAME=VALUE command
    .example-1 = restic will find its repository there
    .example-2 = the exported variables
    .example-3 = sudo keeps your environment (-E)
    .example-4 = a variable for this command only, after sudo
man-unset = (built into bash) forgets a variable: it is no longer passed to programs.
    .synopsis = unset NAME…
    .example-1 = forget a variable
man-promtool = the tool of Prometheus (Debian's prometheus package): it checks configuration and rule files and asks a server questions. check config FILE reads a prometheus.yml (and the rule files it names) and says SUCCESS or FAILED with the line at fault; check rules FILE… checks rule files (their YAML, each rule's PromQL expression, for: durations); check metrics reads metrics in the text format on its input and lints them; query instant SERVER EXPR runs a PromQL query on a server and prints one line per series (labels => value @[timestamp]). Exit code 1 on a failure. Check before every reload: a running Prometheus that rereads a broken file keeps its old configuration and only says so in its journal.
    .synopsis = promtool check config FILE… ; promtool check rules FILE… ; promtool check metrics ; promtool query instant [--time=TIME] SERVER EXPR
    .example-1 = check prometheus.yml and the rule files it names
    .example-2 = check a rule file
    .example-3 = every target's up, at this moment
    .example-4 = the share of time a CPU was idle, over the last 5 minutes
    .example-5 = lint an exporter's metrics
man-prometheus = the Prometheus monitoring server (Debian's prometheus package, service prometheus). It pulls: every scrape_interval it scrapes the targets of /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml (scrape_configs, each job with its static_configs: targets as host:port, extra labels), an HTTP GET on /metrics, and stores each series (a metric name and its labels: job, instance…) with its samples; up is 1 when a scrape worked, 0 otherwise. Every evaluation_interval it evaluates the rules of rule_files (recording rules, and alerting rules: expr, for:, labels, annotations) and sends the firing alerts to the Alertmanagers of alerting:. Its HTTP API, port 9090: /api/v1/query?query=EXPR (POST works too), /api/v1/targets, /api/v1/alerts, /api/v1/rules, /api/v1/status/config, /-/healthy, /-/ready; /-/reload only with --web.enable-lifecycle (ARGS in /etc/default/prometheus). It rereads its configuration on SIGHUP (systemctl reload prometheus); an invalid file is then refused and the old configuration kept (prometheus_config_last_reload_successful is 0).
    .synopsis = prometheus [--config.file=FILE] [--web.enable-lifecycle] [--version]
    .example-1 = the version of the server
    .example-2 = its configuration
    .example-3 = have it reread its configuration and rules (SIGHUP)
    .example-4 = what it says about it
    .example-5 = its targets, their health and their last error
man-prometheus-alertmanager = the Alertmanager (Debian's prometheus-alertmanager package, service prometheus-alertmanager, port 9093): it receives the alerts Prometheus fires, groups them, routes them to receivers and sends notifications. Its configuration, /etc/prometheus/alertmanager.yml: route (receiver, group_by, group_wait: how long a new group waits for others, group_interval: how often a group's changes are sent, repeat_interval: after how long a still firing alert is sent again, 4h by default; routes: nested routes with matchers such as severity="page", the first match wins unless continue: true), receivers (webhook_configs with url, email_configs…), inhibit_rules (an alert that mutes others). A silence mutes the alerts its matchers select, from a start to an end, with a comment and an author (amtool silence add). It rereads its configuration on SIGHUP (systemctl reload prometheus-alertmanager) or POST /-/reload; an invalid file is refused, the old one kept. API: /api/v2/alerts, /api/v2/silences, /api/v2/status.
    .synopsis = prometheus-alertmanager [--config.file=FILE] [--version]
    .example-1 = its configuration
    .example-2 = check it
    .example-3 = have it reread it
    .example-4 = the alerts it holds, through its API
man-prometheus-node-exporter = Prometheus's exporter of machine metrics (Debian's prometheus-node-exporter package, port 9100): CPU (node_cpu_seconds_total, by cpu and mode), load (node_load1), memory (node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes), file systems (node_filesystem_avail_bytes, node_filesystem_size_bytes, by device, fstype and mountpoint), network (node_network_receive_bytes_total)… in the text format, at /metrics. Prometheus must reach its port: open it to the Prometheus server only, since it tells a lot about the machine.
    .synopsis = prometheus-node-exporter [--version]
    .example-1 = what it exposes
    .example-2 = is it running?
    .example-3 = its version
man-amtool = the Alertmanager's command line (Debian's prometheus-alertmanager package). It finds the Alertmanager with --alertmanager.url, or in /etc/amtool/config.yml or ~/.config/amtool/config.yml (alertmanager.url: …). alert query [MATCHERS] lists the active alerts (--silenced and --inhibited add those); silence add MATCHERS --duration=DURATION (or --start and --end, in RFC 3339: 2029-05-19T10:00:00Z) --comment=TEXT creates a silence and prints its ID (a comment is required; the author is your account); silence query lists the active and pending silences (--expired, -q for the IDs only); silence expire ID… ends silences; check-config FILE… checks alertmanager.yml files; config shows the running configuration; config routes draws the routing tree, config routes test LABEL=VALUE… prints the receiver an alert with those labels would reach (--config.file: from a file rather than the running Alertmanager). A matcher is name=value, name!=value, name=~regex or name!~regex; a first word without an operator is the alertname.
    .synopsis = amtool alert query [MATCHERS] ; amtool silence add MATCHERS --duration=D --comment=TEXT ; amtool silence query ; amtool silence expire ID… ; amtool check-config FILE… ; amtool config routes [test LABELS]
    .example-1 = the active alerts
    .example-2 = also those a silence mutes
    .example-3 = the routing tree of the running Alertmanager
    .example-4 = where would a page about a node go?
    .example-5 = check a configuration before reloading it
    .example-6 = silence a machine's alerts for three hours
    .example-7 = the silences in force or to come
    .example-8 = end a silence early
man-jq = a JSON processor: it reads JSON (from its input or files), applies a filter to it and prints the results, indented. . is the input itself; .key a key of an object (."key" if it has unusual characters), .[N] an element of an array, .[] each element; | passes each result to the next filter; , gives several results; {"{"}name: .x, y{"}"} and [ … ] build objects and arrays; "\(.x) \(.y)" builds a string; select(CONDITION) keeps what matches (==, !=, <, >, and, or, not); length, keys, map(f), sort_by(f), to_entries, join(", "), test("regex"), startswith("…"). Exit code 2 or 3 for unreadable input or a filter error, 5 for an error while running.
    .synopsis = jq [-r] [-c] [-n] [-S] [-e] [-R] [-s] FILTER [FILE…]
    .option-r = raw strings: printed without their quotes.
    .option-c = compact: each result on one line.
    .option-n = null input: no input read, the filter runs once on null.
    .option-S = sort the keys of objects.
    .option-e = exit code 1 if the last result is false or null.
    .option-R = raw input: each line is a string (with -s: the whole input, one string).
    .option-s = slurp: all the inputs in one array (with -R: one string).
    .example-1 = Prometheus's answer, indented
    .example-2 = each target, with its job, its health and its last error
    .example-3 = one line per series: its instance and its value
    .example-4 = how many alerts in a saved answer
    .example-5 = a file (a key, with its line breaks) as a JSON string, for an API
man-sleep = waits for a while: a number of seconds, or with a unit (s, m, h, d); several durations add up. In the game, sleep returns at once, and the stories where time matters (metrics, alerts) let that much time go by.
    .synopsis = sleep NUMBER[SUFFIX]…
    .example-1 = wait a minute
    .example-2 = wait five minutes
    .example-3 = an hour and a half
man-kubectl = the command line of Kubernetes (here, k3s's): it talks to the cluster's API server with the credentials of a kubeconfig file (KUBECONFIG, otherwise ~/.kube/config, otherwise /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml, which only root can read). Objects live in namespaces (-n NAME; -A: all of them; the context's namespace by default, kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=NAME). get TYPE [NAME] lists objects (pods, deployments, replicasets, services, endpoints, endpointslices, ingresses, configmaps, secrets, events, nodes, namespaces, all; -o wide, yaml, json, name or jsonpath='{"{"}.spec…{"}"}'; -l app=NAME selects by label; --show-labels; -w follows the changes); describe TYPE NAME shows an object in detail, with its Events at the end; logs POD (or deploy/NAME) prints a container's output (-c CONTAINER, --previous: the container before the last restart, --tail=N, -f to follow); apply -f FILE|DIRECTORY creates or changes objects to match the files (created, configured, unchanged); delete TYPE NAME or -f FILE; edit TYPE NAME opens the live object in the editor; rollout status|history|undo|restart deployment/NAME; scale deployment/NAME --replicas=N; set image deployment/NAME CONTAINER=IMAGE; exec POD -- COMMAND runs a command in a container (-it POD -- sh: a shell); port-forward TYPE/NAME LOCAL:REMOTE forwards a port of your machine while it runs; run NAME --image=IMAGE starts a lone pod; expose deployment NAME --port=N makes a Service; create configmap|secret generic NAME --from-literal=KEY=VALUE --from-file=PATH (--dry-run=client -o yaml to only print it); top pod|node (metrics-server); explain TYPE.FIELD; cluster-info; version; config view|get-contexts. A Deployment owns ReplicaSets (one per version of its pod template), which own the Pods: delete a pod and its ReplicaSet makes another; change the Deployment, not its pods. A container takes a variable from a ConfigMap or a Secret (env: valueFrom: configMapKeyRef or secretKeyRef, with name and key; envFrom: configMapRef or secretRef), read once when it starts: after a change, kubectl rollout restart. A Secret's data are in base64, an encoding, not encryption. A container's resources: requests (what the scheduler reserves on the node; a pod whose requests don't fit stays Pending) and limits (beyond its memory limit, the kernel kills the container: OOMKilled, exit code 137).
    .synopsis = kubectl get|describe|logs|apply|delete|edit|rollout|scale|set|exec|port-forward|run|expose|create|top|explain|config [ARGUMENTS] [-n NAMESPACE] [-A] [-o FORMAT] [-l SELECTOR] [-f FILE]
    .option-n = the namespace (--namespace).
    .option-A = every namespace (--all-namespaces).
    .option-o = output format: wide, yaml, json, name, jsonpath=TEMPLATE (--output).
    .option-l = only the objects whose labels match: app=booking, tier!=db, env in (a,b) (--selector).
    .option-f = a manifest file, or a directory of them (--filename); for logs, follow (--follow).
    .option-w = after listing, follow the changes, until Ctrl-C (--watch).
    .option-c = the container, for a pod that has several (--container).
    .option-p = logs: the previous instance of the container, the one that crashed (--previous).
    .option-i = exec: pass your input to the container (--stdin); with -t, an interactive shell.
    .option-t = exec: a terminal for the container (--tty).
    .example-1 = the pods of the swim namespace, their state and restarts
    .example-2 = every pod of the cluster, with its address and node
    .example-3 = a pod in detail: its containers, their state, and its Events
    .example-4 = what the container printed before it crashed
    .example-5 = create or update everything a directory of manifests describes
    .example-6 = wait for a Deployment's rollout to finish (or fail)
    .example-7 = which pods a Service sends traffic to
    .example-8 = the log of Traefik, k3s's ingress controller
    .example-9 = make swim the namespace of the current context
    .example-10 = a Deployment as the API server holds it, status included
    .example-11 = a shell inside one of a Deployment's containers
    .example-12 = a Secret, printed without being created (drop --dry-run to create it)
    .example-13 = what a Secret holds: base64 is an encoding, not encryption
    .example-14 = new pods for a Deployment, one by one (they read their variables again)
    .example-15 = the memory and CPU each pod uses now
    .example-16 = the node: its capacity, what it can give (Allocatable), and what the pods request
    .example-17 = a Deployment's revisions and their CHANGE-CAUSE
    .example-18 = go back to a revision of a Deployment
man-k3s = a lightweight Kubernetes in a single binary (https://docs.k3s.io/), run as the k3s service (systemctl status k3s, journalctl -u k3s). It brings containerd, the Traefik ingress controller (ports 80 and 443 of the node, through ServiceLB), CoreDNS, the local-path provisioner and metrics-server, in the kube-system namespace; pods get addresses in 10.42.0.0/16, Services in 10.43.0.0/16. Its kubeconfig is /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml (readable by root only); kubectl is a link to k3s. k3s kubectl runs the same kubectl; k3s --version prints its version; k3s check-config checks the kernel's options.
    .synopsis = k3s kubectl ARGUMENTS… ; k3s --version ; k3s check-config
    .example-1 = the version of k3s
    .example-2 = kubectl, with the server's own credentials
    .example-3 = is the service running?
    .example-4 = what it says about itself
man-base64 = encodes its input (or a file) in base64, or decodes it with -d. Base64 writes any bytes with 64 printable characters: it is an encoding, not encryption; anyone can decode it. A Kubernetes Secret keeps its values in base64 (data:). Careful: echo adds a newline, which gets encoded too (echo -n doesn't).
    .synopsis = base64 [-d] [-w COLUMNS] [FILE]
    .option-d = decode (--decode).
    .option-w = wrap the encoded lines after COLUMNS characters (76 by default; 0: no wrapping).
    .example-1 = encode a word, without the newline echo would add
    .example-2 = decode it
    .example-3 = encode a file on a single line
man-printf = prints text in a format, like the C function: printf "%s is %d years old\n" Ada 36. Each % takes the next argument: %s a text, %d an integer, %f a decimal number (%.2f: two digits after the point), %x in hexadecimal, %c a character, %b a text whose escapes are read, %% a % sign; a width pads (%5d, %-10s on the left, %05d with zeros). If arguments remain, the format starts again. Unlike echo, printf adds no new line by itself: end the format with \n. A shell built-in (help printf, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = printf format [argument…]
    .example-1 = one argument per line
    .example-2 = aligned columns
    .example-3 = a number with two decimals
man-true = does nothing, successfully (exit code 0). Useful in a list: command || true never fails. A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = true
    .example-1 = always succeeds
man-false = does nothing, unsuccessfully (exit code 1). A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = false
    .example-1 = always fails: the || runs
man-type = tells how the shell reads a name: an alias (ls is aliased to `ls --color=auto', from Debian's ~/.bashrc), a keyword (if, for), a shell built-in (cd, echo) or a program (with its path); "not found" otherwise. -t gives one word (alias, keyword, builtin, file), -p only a program's path, -a every meaning. A shell built-in (help type, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = type [-a] [-t] [-p] name…
    .option-t = one word: alias, keyword, builtin or file.
    .option-p = the program's path (nothing for a builtin).
    .option-a = every meaning of the name.
    .example-1 = what is ls? and cd?
    .example-2 = just the kind
man-command = command -v name prints what the shell would run: a program's path, a built-in's name, an alias's definition; nothing (exit code 1) if the name is unknown: the portable way to check a command exists in a script (command -v rsync || echo missing). command name arguments runs the program without its alias. A shell built-in (help command, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = command -v name… ; command -V name… ; command name [argument…]
    .option-v = what would run, in one word or path.
    .option-V = the same, in a sentence (like type).
    .example-1 = is rsync installed?
    .example-2 = ls without its colour alias
man-rmdir = removes an empty place (remove directory): a place that still holds something stays ("Directory not empty"). -p also removes the parents named in the path, as long as they become empty.
    .synopsis = rmdir [-p] [-v] place…
    .option-p = also removes each parent of the path, if empty (parents).
    .option-v = says each place removed (verbose).
    .example-1 = remove the empty place old
    .example-2 = remove tmp, then 1, then releases, if they are empty
man-xargs = builds command lines from its input: each word read (separated by blanks or new lines) becomes an argument of the command. find … | xargs grep error runs grep on all the files found. -n N puts at most N arguments per command; -I {"{"}{"}"} runs the command once per line, {"{"}{"}"} replaced by the line; -0 reads names separated by a NUL byte (find -print0), the safe way for names with spaces; -r runs nothing if the input is empty; -t shows each command before running it. Its exit code is 123 if a command failed.
    .synopsis = xargs [-0] [-n N] [-L N] [-I string] [-r] [-t] [command [argument…]]
    .option-0 = items separated by NUL (with find -print0).
    .option-n = at most N arguments per command.
    .option-I = one command per line, this string replaced by the line.
    .option-L = one command every N lines.
    .option-r = nothing at all if the input is empty (no-run-if-empty).
    .option-t = shows each command before running it.
    .option-d = items separated by this character.
    .example-1 = details of the compressed logs found
    .example-2 = delete the .tmp files, even with spaces in their names
    .example-3 = ping each machine of a list, once
    .example-4 = a .bak copy of each configuration
man-env = shows the environment: the variables every program receives (HOME, PATH, USER…), one NAME=value per line. env NAME=value command runs the command with a changed environment; -u NAME removes a variable, -i starts from an empty one.
    .synopsis = env [-i] [-u NAME] [NAME=value…] [command [argument…]]
    .option-i = starts from an empty environment.
    .option-u = removes this variable.
    .example-1 = the whole environment
    .example-2 = just the PATH
    .example-3 = the date in the C language, for this command only
man-printenv = prints the value of environment variables (only their values), or the whole environment without a name. Exit code 1 if a variable is not in the environment (a variable not exported isn't).
    .synopsis = printenv [NAME…]
    .example-1 = your home directory
    .example-2 = the whole environment
man-alias = gives a command a short name, for this session: alias ll='ls -la', then ll. Without an argument, lists the aliases: on Debian, ~/.bashrc already defines ls='ls --color=auto' and grep='grep --color=auto'. An alias only replaces the first word of a command; to keep it, write it in ~/.bashrc. A shell built-in (help alias, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = alias [name[='value']…]
    .example-1 = the aliases already defined
    .example-2 = ll for a long listing, hidden files included
    .example-3 = what ll is now
man-unalias = forgets an alias of this session; -a forgets them all. A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = unalias [-a] name…
    .option-a = forgets every alias.
    .example-1 = forget ll
man-seq = prints a sequence of numbers, one per line: seq 5 (1 to 5), seq 2 5 (2 to 5), seq 0 10 100 (from 0 to 100 by tens). -w pads them with zeros to the same width (01 02 … 10), -s chooses the separator (seq -s, 3 gives 1,2,3), -f a printf format. Often the list a for loop goes through: for i in $(seq 1 3); do …; done (bash's {"{"}1..3{"}"} does the same without a program).
    .synopsis = seq [-w] [-s separator] [-f format] [first [increment]] last
    .option-w = pads the numbers with zeros to the same width.
    .option-s = separator between the numbers (a newline by default).
    .option-f = a printf format for each number (%g, %.2f…).
    .example-1 = 1 to 5
    .example-2 = from 0 to 100, by tens
    .example-3 = three tries, numbered
man-read = reads one line of input into variables: read name, then $name. With several names, each takes a word, the last one takes the rest of the line; without a name, the line goes into $REPLY. Its input is the keyboard, a file (read line < file) or a loop's input: while read line; do …; done < file goes through a file line by line, as does cmd | while read line; do …; done (but the loop then runs in a subshell: the variables it sets are lost after done). -r keeps the backslashes as they are (always use it for files), -p shows a prompt. Exit code 1 at the end of the input, which ends the while loop. A shell built-in (help read, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = read [-r] [-p prompt] [name…]
    .option-r = backslashes are ordinary characters.
    .option-p = shows this prompt before reading (from the keyboard).
    .example-1 = ask a question, keep the answer
    .example-2 = each line of a file
    .example-3 = the first word, then the rest
man-break = leaves the for, while or until loop it is in; break N leaves N nested loops. Outside a loop, bash says it is only meaningful in one. A shell built-in (help break, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = break [N]
    .example-1 = stop at the first file that is too big
man-set = changes the shell's options, or its positional parameters. -e (errexit): the script stops at the first command that fails, except in an if or while test, on the left of && and ||, or after !; -u (nounset): an unset variable is an error that stops the script; -x (xtrace): each command is written, expanded, before it runs (+ echo hello); -o pipefail (bash only): a pipeline fails if any of its commands fails, not only the last one. + instead of - turns an option off. set -- a b c sets $1, $2, $3. set -o lists the options; set alone, the variables. At the top of a bash script: set -euo pipefail. dash refuses -o pipefail ("Illegal option") and stops. A shell built-in (help set, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = set [-eux] [+eux] [-o option] [--] [argument...]
    .example-1 = the safe start of a bash script
    .example-2 = show each command before it runs
    .example-3 = new positional parameters: $1 is alpha
    .example-4 = the options and their state
man-shift = moves the positional parameters to the left: $2 becomes $1, $3 becomes $2, and $# goes down by one; shift N by N. To go through a script's arguments one by one. With nothing left to shift, bash fails quietly (code 1), dash stops the script ("can't shift that many"). A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = shift [N]
    .example-1 = drop $1
    .example-2 = each argument, one by one
man-local = gives a function its own variable: local name=value hides the variable of the same name until the function returns, then the old value comes back. Outside a function, an error. bash and dash know it (POSIX doesn't, Solaris's /bin/sh neither). A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = local name[=value]...
    .example-1 = a variable that belongs to the function
man-return = ends a function, with an exit code (that of the last command, without N): the caller reads it in $? or tests it with if. In a file read by . (source), ends the reading. Outside a function, bash refuses ("can only `return' from a function or sourced script"), dash ends the script. A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = return [N]
    .example-1 = fail early when the file is missing
man-trap = runs commands when the shell receives a signal or, with EXIT, when the script ends, whatever the way (the last command, exit, set -e): the place to clean up temporary files. trap alone lists the traps, trap - EXIT removes one. Put the action in single quotes: its variables are read when it runs. In this game, only EXIT happens (no signal reaches a script). A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = trap 'commands' EXIT|INT|TERM|HUP… ; trap - signal ; trap
    .example-1 = delete the temporary file at the end, whatever happens
    .example-2 = the traps set
    .example-3 = remove the EXIT trap
man-getopts = reads a script's options, one per call, for a while loop: getopts vf: opt puts the next option's letter in $opt (and its value in $OPTARG for an option followed by : in the list); it fails when there are none left. $OPTIND is the index of the next argument: shift $((OPTIND - 1)) leaves the operands. An unknown option gives ? (and a message: "illegal option -- z" for bash, "Illegal option -z" for dash); a list starting with : keeps quiet. A shell built-in, the same in every POSIX sh.
    .synopsis = getopts optstring name [argument...]
    .example-1 = -v and -f file, in a loop
    .example-2 = after the loop, keep only the operands
man-source = reads a file of commands and runs it in the current shell (not in a child, like ./script): its variables, functions and cd stay. The POSIX name is "." (a dot): . ./settings.sh. Without a /, the file is looked for in the PATH (bash also looks here; dash doesn't: ". settings.sh" fails there, write ./settings.sh). source exists only in bash: dash says "source: not found". A shell built-in.
    .synopsis = source file [argument...] ; . file
    .example-1 = reread your bash settings
    .example-2 = load variables, the POSIX way
man-continue = skips the rest of this round of the loop and goes on with the next one; continue N with the next round of the N-th enclosing loop. A shell built-in (help continue, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = continue [N]
    .example-1 = skip the empty files
man-jobs = lists the jobs of this shell: the commands started in the background with & (sleep 60 &) or stopped with Ctrl-Z. Each has a number, [1], [2]…: %1 names it for fg, bg, kill and wait; + marks the current job (the last one), - the previous one. Running, Stopped, Done (it ended: bash says so after your next command), Terminated (kill), Killed (kill -9). -l adds the PIDs, -p shows only them. A shell built-in (help jobs, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = jobs [-l] [-p] [job…]
    .option-l = with each job's PID.
    .option-p = only the PIDs.
    .example-1 = start a job in the background, then list the jobs
    .example-2 = with their PIDs
man-fg = brings a job back to the foreground: it takes the terminal again (Ctrl-C stops it, Ctrl-Z stops it again). Without an argument, the current job (+); %2 for job 2. A shell built-in (help fg, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = fg [job]
    .example-1 = the current job
    .example-2 = job 2
man-bg = lets a job stopped with Ctrl-Z run on, in the background, as if it had been started with &. A shell built-in (help bg, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = bg [job]
    .example-1 = the stopped job runs on, in the background
man-wait = waits for the jobs in the background to end (all of them, or the one given) and gives back the exit code of the last one; a job that never ends (tail -f) makes wait wait until Ctrl-C. A shell built-in (help wait, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = wait [job | PID…]
    .example-1 = wait for every job
    .example-2 = wait for job 1
man-disown = removes a job from the shell's table: jobs no longer lists it, and closing the session no longer sends it the hangup signal (HUP). -a for every job. A shell built-in (help disown, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = disown [-a] [job…]
    .option-a = every job.
    .example-1 = keep the current job running after logout
man-nohup = runs a command that ignores the hangup signal (HUP): it keeps running when the session closes (the SSH connection drops). If its output would go to the terminal, it is appended to nohup.out (in this place, or in your home). Usually followed by &: nohup ./long-job.sh &. For anything that must survive, tmux or a systemd service is better.
    .synopsis = nohup command [argument…]
    .example-1 = a long job that survives logout, in the background
    .example-2 = its output, kept in a file of your choice
man-watch = runs a command every 2 seconds and shows its output full screen, until Ctrl-C: watch df -h, watch -n 5 'ls -l /var/backups'. -n chooses the interval in seconds, -t hides the title line (the interval, the command, the machine and the time). In this game, the screen is redrawn as the machine's time passes, when the output changes.
    .synopsis = watch [-n seconds] [-t] command
    .option-n = the interval, in seconds (2 by default).
    .option-t = no title line.
    .example-1 = the disks' free space, every 2 seconds
    .example-2 = a directory, every 5 seconds

man-gzip = compresses files (GNU zip): gzip file replaces file with file.gz, smaller (a log takes about a third of its size; a photo or a video gains nothing, they are already compressed), and keeps its rights and date. -d decompresses (like gunzip), -k keeps the original, -c writes to the standard output (gzip -c f > f.gz), -l shows the sizes and the ratio, -t checks the file is whole, -r goes through a directory, -1 (fast) to -9 (best). Not to the screen: compressed data is unreadable (cat of a .gz shows garbage), read it with zcat, zgrep or zless. logrotate compresses the old logs this way (syslog.2.gz).
    .synopsis = gzip [-cdfklqrtv19] [file…]
    .option-c = writes to the standard output, keeps the files.
    .option-d = decompresses.
    .option-f = forces: overwrites, writes to a terminal.
    .option-k = keeps the original (keep).
    .option-l = lists the compressed and uncompressed sizes, and the ratio.
    .option-r = goes through the directories (recursive).
    .option-t = tests that the compressed file is whole.
    .option-v = says what it did and the ratio.
    .option-q = says nothing of the warnings.
    .option-1 = compresses faster (less).
    .option-9 = compresses best (slower).
    .example-1 = compress, keeping the original
    .example-2 = what a rotated log takes, compressed and not
    .example-3 = compress into another file
    .example-4 = compress everything in a directory, as much as possible
man-gunzip = decompresses gzip files: gunzip file.gz gives back file (and removes the .gz, unless -k). The same program as gzip -d: .tgz becomes .tar. A name without .gz is refused (unknown suffix); gunzip x finds x.gz.
    .synopsis = gunzip [-cfkltv] file.gz…
    .option-c = writes to the standard output, keeps the .gz.
    .option-f = overwrites the decompressed file if it exists.
    .option-k = keeps the .gz.
    .option-l = lists the sizes.
    .option-t = tests the file.
    .option-v = says what it did.
    .example-1 = decompress (the .gz disappears)
    .example-2 = decompress and keep the .gz
    .example-3 = check a compressed backup is whole
man-zcat = shows the content of gzip files, without decompressing them on the disk: zcat /var/log/syslog.2.gz (gzip -dc). The way to read rotated logs, often through a pipe: zcat access.log.*.gz | grep 404. zcat x reads x.gz when x doesn't exist; a file that isn't compressed is refused (not in gzip format).
    .synopsis = zcat [-flv] file.gz…
    .option-f = shows a file that isn't compressed as it is.
    .option-l = lists the sizes.
    .option-v = says what it reads.
    .example-1 = read a rotated log
    .example-2 = search through every rotated log
    .example-3 = the start of a compressed dump
man-zgrep = grep on compressed files: zgrep pattern file.gz searches the decompressed text, with grep's options (-i, -c, -l, -n, -v…); a file that isn't compressed is read as it is, so zgrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log* goes through the current log and the rotated ones. With several files, each line starts with its file's name (-h: without).
    .synopsis = zgrep [grep's options] pattern [file…]
    .option-i = ignores case.
    .option-c = counts the matching lines, per file.
    .option-l = only the names of the files that match.
    .option-h = without the files' names.
    .option-H = with the file's name, even for one file.
    .option-n = numbers the lines.
    .option-v = the lines that don't match.
    .option-E = extended regular expression.
    .option-w = whole words only.
    .example-1 = failed SSH logins in the rotated logs
    .example-2 = how many errors in a compressed log
    .example-3 = which compressed logs mention this address
man-zless = less on compressed files: zless file.gz shows the decompressed text, page by page. On Debian, less itself reads a .gz (lesspipe, set up by ~/.bashrc).
    .synopsis = zless file…
    .example-1 = page through a rotated log
man-zmore = more on compressed files: zmore file.gz shows the decompressed text, a screen at a time.
    .synopsis = zmore file…
    .example-1 = read a rotated log, a screen at a time
man-zdiff = diff on compressed files: zdiff a.gz b compares their decompressed texts, with diff's options (-u…). With one file x.gz, compares it with x.
    .synopsis = zdiff [diff's options] file1 [file2]
    .example-1 = what changed between a compressed copy and the current file
    .example-2 = a compressed file against its uncompressed version
man-bzip2 = compresses files with the Burrows-Wheeler algorithm: bzip2 file replaces file with file.bz2, a little smaller than gzip's, more slowly. -d decompresses (bunzip2), -k keeps the original, -c writes to the standard output, -t tests. No -l nor -r. tar -j uses it.
    .synopsis = bzip2 [-cdfkqtvz19] [file…]
    .option-c = writes to the standard output.
    .option-d = decompresses.
    .option-f = overwrites.
    .option-k = keeps the original.
    .option-t = tests the file.
    .option-v = says the ratio.
    .option-z = compresses (the default).
    .option-q = quiet.
    .option-1 = smallest blocks (100k).
    .option-9 = biggest blocks (900k, the default).
    .example-1 = compress, keeping the original
    .example-2 = decompress
    .example-3 = compress into another file
man-bunzip2 = decompresses bzip2 files: bunzip2 file.bz2 gives back file (bzip2 -d).
    .synopsis = bunzip2 [-cfktv] file.bz2…
    .option-c = writes to the standard output.
    .option-f = overwrites.
    .option-k = keeps the .bz2.
    .option-t = tests the file.
    .option-v = says what it did.
    .example-1 = decompress
man-bzcat = shows the content of bzip2 files (bzip2 -dc).
    .synopsis = bzcat file.bz2…
    .example-1 = the start of a compressed dump
man-xz = compresses files in the xz format (LZMA2): xz file replaces file with file.xz, the smallest of the three, the slowest to compress. -d decompresses (unxz), -k keeps the original, -c writes to the standard output, -l lists the sizes and the ratio (compressed / uncompressed), -t tests, -0 to -9. Debian's packages and many source archives are .tar.xz; tar -J uses it.
    .synopsis = xz [-cdfklqtvz09] [file…]
    .option-c = writes to the standard output.
    .option-d = decompresses.
    .option-f = overwrites.
    .option-k = keeps the original.
    .option-l = lists the sizes and the ratio.
    .option-t = tests the file.
    .option-v = says the ratio.
    .option-z = compresses (the default).
    .option-q = quiet.
    .option-0 = compresses faster (less).
    .option-9 = compresses best (much slower).
    .example-1 = compress, keeping the original
    .example-2 = what is in an .xz archive
    .example-3 = decompress
    .example-4 = compress as much as possible, into another file
man-unxz = decompresses xz files: unxz file.xz gives back file (xz -d).
    .synopsis = unxz [-cfktv] file.xz…
    .option-c = writes to the standard output.
    .option-f = overwrites.
    .option-k = keeps the .xz.
    .option-t = tests the file.
    .option-v = says what it did.
    .example-1 = decompress an archive
man-xzcat = shows the content of xz files (xz -dc).
    .synopsis = xzcat file.xz…
    .example-1 = read a compressed file
man-zip = puts files into a .zip archive (Info-ZIP), the format Windows opens without help: zip archive.zip files…; -r takes the directories with their content. Each file is compressed alone (deflated) or stored when it gains nothing. The archive gets .zip if its name has no suffix; an existing archive is updated. Not installed by default on Debian: apt install zip.
    .synopsis = zip [-rq] archive[.zip] file…
    .option-r = takes the directories with their content (recursive).
    .option-q = says nothing (quiet).
    .example-1 = a directory in a zip archive
    .example-2 = two files in a zip archive
man-unzip = extracts a .zip archive (Info-ZIP): unzip archive.zip extracts here, -d into a directory (created if needed), -l lists without extracting (sizes, dates, names). A file that already exists makes it ask (replace? [y]es, [n]o, [A]ll, [N]one); -o overwrites without asking, -n never overwrites. Not installed by default on Debian: apt install unzip.
    .synopsis = unzip [-l] [-o|-n] [-q] archive[.zip] [-d directory]
    .option-l = lists the content.
    .option-d = extracts into this directory.
    .option-o = overwrites without asking.
    .option-n = never overwrites.
    .option-q = quiet.
    .example-1 = what is in the archive
    .example-2 = extract into a directory
    .example-3 = extract here, overwriting

man-basename = the last part of a path, its name without the directories: basename /var/log/syslog gives syslog. A second argument, a suffix, is removed too: basename site.tar.gz .tar.gz gives site. -a takes several names, -s SUFFIX removes the suffix from each. Often in scripts: "$(basename "$f" .log)".
    .synopsis = basename NAME [SUFFIX] ; basename -a [-s SUFFIX] NAME…
    .option-a = several names, one per line.
    .option-s = the suffix to remove (implies -a).
    .example-1 = the name of a log
    .example-2 = without its suffix
    .example-3 = the name of each log, without .log
man-dirname = the path without its last part: dirname /etc/nginx/nginx.conf gives /etc/nginx; a name without a slash gives . (the current directory). Often with realpath, to reach a script's own directory.
    .synopsis = dirname NAME…
    .example-1 = the directory of a file
    .example-2 = go to the directory a script is in
man-realpath = the absolute, canonical path of a file: every symbolic link followed, . and .. resolved (like readlink -f). The last part may be missing; -e wants it to exist, -m accepts anything missing, -s doesn't follow links.
    .synopsis = realpath [-e|-m] [-s] FILE…
    .option-e = every part must exist.
    .option-m = no part needs to exist.
    .option-s = doesn't follow symbolic links.
    .example-1 = where a relative path really leads
    .example-2 = the file a link of sites-enabled points to
man-yes = writes y (or the word given) again and again, until the program reading it stops: yes | head -3. Used to answer yes to every question of a command that asks some (better: its own option, apt-get -y). On the screen it never ends: Ctrl-C.
    .synopsis = yes [WORD]
    .example-1 = three y
    .example-2 = answer every question (apt-get -y does it better)
man-tac = cat backwards: the lines of a file from the last to the first. Handy to read a log from the most recent line.
    .synopsis = tac [FILE…]
    .example-1 = the latest lines of a log first
    .example-2 = a file upside down
man-nl = numbers the lines of a file. By default only those with text (-b t); -b a numbers them all, -b n none; -n ln, rn or rz place the number (left, right, right with zeros), -w its width, -s the separator after it (a tab).
    .synopsis = nl [-b a|t|n] [-n ln|rn|rz] [-w WIDTH] [-s SEP] [FILE…]
    .option-b = which lines: a (all), t (with text), n (none).
    .option-n = the number's format: ln, rn, rz.
    .option-w = the number's width (6).
    .option-s = what follows the number (a tab).
    .option-v = the first number (1).
    .option-i = the step between numbers (1).
    .example-1 = a script with its line numbers
    .example-2 = every line numbered, empty ones too
man-column = lays text out in columns. -t makes a table: each line a row, cells split on blanks (or on the characters of -s), aligned on the widest, two spaces between. Without -t, fills columns like ls does. util-linux's column, in the bsdextrautils package.
    .synopsis = column [-t] [-s SEPARATORS] [-o OUTPUT_SEPARATOR] [FILE…]
    .option-t = a table, aligned.
    .option-s = the characters that separate the cells.
    .option-o = what separates the columns in the output (two spaces).
    .example-1 = the mounts, aligned
    .example-2 = the accounts, field by field
    .example-3 = each account and its shell
man-paste = puts files side by side: line 1 of each, separated by a tab, then line 2… -d chooses the separators (one character after another), -s puts each file on one line (paste -sd+ sizes.txt | bc adds a list).
    .synopsis = paste [-s] [-d LIST] FILE…
    .option-d = the separators (a tab by default).
    .option-s = each file on a single line (serial).
    .example-1 = two lists side by side
    .example-2 = separated by commas, like a CSV
    .example-3 = a list joined with +
man-md5sum = the MD5 fingerprint of files (32 hexadecimal characters), and -c to check a list of them, like sha256sum. MD5 is broken: anyone can make two files with the same fingerprint. Enough to spot an accidental change, never to trust a download: use sha256sum.
    .synopsis = md5sum FILE… ; md5sum -c LIST
    .option-c = check the files of a list.
    .example-1 = a file's fingerprint
    .example-2 = check them
man-sha1sum = the SHA-1 fingerprint of files (40 hexadecimal characters), and -c to check a list. SHA-1 is no longer safe against someone who wants to cheat (collisions since 2017): prefer sha256sum.
    .synopsis = sha1sum FILE… ; sha1sum -c LIST
    .option-c = check the files of a list.
    .example-1 = a file's fingerprint
    .example-2 = check them
man-cmp = compares two files byte by byte and says where they first differ (differ: char N, line L); nothing if they are identical. Exit code 0 identical, 1 different, 2 trouble. -s says nothing: only the exit code, for a script. For texts, diff shows what changed.
    .synopsis = cmp [-s] FILE1 [FILE2]
    .option-s = silent: only the exit code.
    .example-1 = is the copy the same as the original?
    .example-2 = in a script
man-whereis = where a command's program and its manual page are: whereis ls gives ls: /usr/bin/ls /usr/share/man/man1/ls.1.gz. A command not found gives its name alone.
    .synopsis = whereis NAME…
    .example-1 = the program and the page of ls
    .example-2 = is nginx installed?
man-more = shows a text a screen at a time, the old pager (less does more: going back, searching). Several files: each announced between lines of colons.
    .synopsis = more [FILE…]
    .example-1 = read a long file
    .example-2 = the kernel's messages, page by page
man-vmstat = a summary of the machine's activity (virtual memory statistics): processes waiting (r) or blocked (b), memory free and in cache, swap in and out, disk blocks read and written, interrupts and context switches, and the processor's share (us user, sy system, id idle, wa waiting for the disk, st stolen by the hypervisor, gu guest). -s lists the memory counters.
    .synopsis = vmstat [-s] [delay [count]]
    .option-s = the memory counters, one per line.
    .option-w = wide output.
    .example-1 = the activity at a glance
    .example-2 = the memory, counter by counter
man-lscpu = describes the processor: architecture, number of CPUs, model, cores and threads, caches, virtualisation.
    .synopsis = lscpu
    .example-1 = the processor
    .example-2 = only its model
man-sync = writes to the disks what the system still keeps in memory (the page cache). Before pulling out a USB key, or after a big copy, before cutting the power.
    .synopsis = sync [FILE…]
    .example-1 = write everything now
    .example-2 = a copy really on the key
man-timeout = runs a command and stops it if it lasts longer than the given time (5, 10s, 2m, 1h): exit code 124 when time ran out, 125 if timeout itself failed, 127 if the command doesn't exist. -s chooses the signal (TERM by default; KILL gives 137).
    .synopsis = timeout [-s SIGNAL] [-k DELAY] DURATION COMMAND [ARGUMENT…]
    .option-s = the signal sent when time is up.
    .option-k = then KILL after this delay, if it is still running.
    .example-1 = stopped after 5 seconds
    .example-2 = no more than 10 seconds for a request
    .example-3 = a job killed after a minute
man-install = copies files and sets their rights, owner and group in one go: install -m 755 script /usr/local/bin/name. -d creates directories (with -m, -o, -g), -D creates the destination's directories. The usual way to put a program or a key in place without a separate chmod.
    .synopsis = install [-m MODE] [-o OWNER] [-g GROUP] [-D] SOURCE… DEST ; install -d [-m MODE] DIRECTORY…
    .option-m = the rights (755, u=rw…); rwxr-xr-x by default.
    .option-o = the owner (root only).
    .option-g = the group (root only).
    .option-d = creates directories.
    .option-D = creates the destination's directories first.
    .example-1 = put a script in place, executable
    .example-2 = a site's directory, for www-data only
    .example-3 = a key readable by its owner alone
man-chattr = changes a file's attributes on ext4 (not its rights): +i makes it immutable (nobody, not even root, may change, rename or delete it, nor create anything in an immutable directory), +a append-only (it can only grow: >>, logs). -i, -a remove them. Setting i or a takes root. Handy to protect a file another program would rewrite (/etc/resolv.conf); a trap when you forget it: "Operation not permitted" even for root, look with lsattr.
    .synopsis = chattr [-R] [+-=][aAcdeijsStTu] FILE…
    .example-1 = nobody may change it any more
    .example-2 = it may again
    .example-3 = a log that can only grow
man-lsattr = shows the ext4 attributes of files (set by chattr): one letter per column, i (immutable), a (append only), e (extents, on every ext4 file). On a directory, those of its files; -d the directory itself.
    .synopsis = lsattr [-dRa] [FILE…]
    .option-d = the directory itself, not its content.
    .option-R = goes through the directories.
    .option-a = includes the hidden files.
    .example-1 = is it immutable?
    .example-2 = a directory's attributes
man-shutdown = stops or restarts the machine (root): shutdown now powers it off, -r now restarts it, +10 or 23:30 schedules it (and warns the connected users), -c cancels. Over ssh, the connection drops: on a server far away, a poweroff means someone has to press the button.
    .synopsis = shutdown [-r|-h|-P|-H] [now|+MINUTES|HH:MM] ; shutdown -c
    .option-r = restarts (reboot).
    .option-h = powers off (like -P).
    .option-P = powers off.
    .option-H = halts without powering off.
    .option-c = cancels a scheduled shutdown.
    .example-1 = restart now
    .example-2 = power off in ten minutes
    .example-3 = cancel it
    .example-4 = power off now
man-poweroff = powers the machine off at once (root), like shutdown now. Over ssh, the connection drops, and on a real server nobody brings it back but the hosting provider's console.
    .synopsis = poweroff
    .example-1 = power off
man-halt = halts the machine (root): the system stops, without necessarily cutting the power. Like poweroff, the session drops.
    .synopsis = halt
    .example-1 = halt the system

man-wget = downloads a URL into a file named after it (index.html for a directory; an existing name gets .1), telling everything on the way: resolving the name, connecting, the server's answer (200 OK, 404 Not Found…), the size, the progress bar. -O FILE chooses the file, -O - writes to the standard output, -q says nothing: wget -qO- URL is curl's habit. --spider only checks the URL answers. Exit code 4 for a network failure, 8 for an error answer from the server.
    .synopsis = wget [-q] [-O FILE] [--spider] URL
    .option-O = the file to write (- for the standard output).
    .option-q = quiet: no log.
    .example-1 = download a page
    .example-2 = into a chosen file
    .example-3 = the answer on the screen, nothing else
    .example-4 = does the URL answer?
man-nslookup = asks a DNS server for a name's address: the server used (Server:, from /etc/resolv.conf, or the one given after the name), then the answer (Name:, Address:), or ** server can't find NAME: NXDOMAIN. Like dig and host, it never reads /etc/hosts: a name there may be unknown to the DNS. Older than dig, still everywhere (Windows too).
    .synopsis = nslookup NAME [SERVER]
    .example-1 = the address of a name
    .example-2 = asked to a given server
man-lpstat = shows the state of CUPS: the scheduler (-r), the default destination (-d), each queue's device (-v), whether it accepts jobs (-a), whether it is enabled or disabled and why (-p), and the jobs (-o). -t shows all of it. A queue name after -a, -o, -p or -v limits the list to it. -W completed -o shows finished jobs (the history CUPS keeps). Without an option: your own jobs.
    .synopsis = lpstat [-t] [-r] [-d] [-a [dest]] [-p [dest]] [-v [dest]] [-o [dest]] [-W completed|not-completed|all] [-u user] [-l]
    .option-a = whether each queue accepts jobs.
    .option-d = the system's default destination.
    .option-l = long listing (with -p: description, location, interface, allowed users).
    .option-o = the jobs, as QUEUE-ID, owner, size in bytes and date.
    .option-p = each queue: idle, printing, or disabled since a date, with the reason.
    .option-r = whether the scheduler (cupsd) runs.
    .option-s = a summary: the default and the devices.
    .option-t = everything: -r, -d, -v, -a, -p and -o.
    .option-u = the jobs of these users.
    .option-v = the device (URI) of each queue.
    .option-W = which jobs -o shows: not-completed (default), completed or all.
    .example-1 = the whole state of the print server
    .example-2 = is the office queue enabled, and why not
    .example-3 = the jobs waiting
    .example-4 = the jobs finished (history)
    .example-5 = where each queue sends its jobs
    .example-6 = the default destination
man-lp = sends files to a printer (a CUPS destination). -d chooses the queue (otherwise $LPDEST, $PRINTER, lpoptions' default, then the server's); without a file, lp prints its standard input. Answers with the job's id: request id is QUEUE-ID.
    .synopsis = lp [-d destination] [-n copies] [-t title] [-o option=value] [file…]
    .option-d = the destination (queue).
    .option-n = the number of copies.
    .option-t = the job's title (by default, the file's name).
    .option-o = a job option (sides=two-sided-long-edge, media=A4…).
    .option-s = silent: no "request id" line.
    .example-1 = print a file on the office printer
    .example-2 = two copies, with a title
    .example-3 = print a line of text
man-lpr = sends files to a printer, the Berkeley way (the same CUPS underneath). -P chooses the queue. Silent on success.
    .synopsis = lpr [-P destination] [-# copies] [-T title] [file…]
    .option-P = the destination (queue).
    .option-- = the number of copies (-# 3).
    .option-T = the job's title.
    .example-1 = print a file on the office printer
    .example-2 = three copies on the default printer
man-lpq = shows a queue's state and its jobs, in order, the Berkeley way: Rank (active, 1st, 2nd…), Owner, Job, File(s), Total Size. "is not ready": the queue is disabled.
    .synopsis = lpq [-P destination] [-a] [-l] [job|user…]
    .option-P = the queue (otherwise the default one).
    .option-a = the jobs of every queue.
    .option-l = long listing.
    .example-1 = the default queue
    .example-2 = the office queue
    .example-3 = every queue
man-cancel = cancels print jobs, by id (117 or office-117), or the current job of a queue. -a cancels every job (of a queue, or of all of them): everyone's, for an administrator. -x also purges the jobs' documents, which CUPS otherwise keeps for PreserveJobFiles (a day by default); it purges those of a job already finished too. Only the owner or an administrator may cancel a job (the policy's Cancel-Job limit).
    .synopsis = cancel [-a] [-x] [-u user] [id|destination…]
    .option-a = every job (of the queues named, or of all queues).
    .option-u = the jobs of this user.
    .option-x = purge the documents too.
    .example-1 = cancel job 117
    .example-2 = the same, with the queue's name
    .example-3 = cancel it and purge its document from the spool
    .example-4 = Sven's jobs on the office queue
    .example-5 = every job of the office queue (everyone's!)
man-lprm = cancels print jobs, the Berkeley way: by id, or the current job; "-" cancels all your jobs (all of them, for an administrator).
    .synopsis = lprm [-P destination] [-] [id…]
    .option-P = the queue.
    .example-1 = cancel job 117
    .example-2 = on the office queue
    .example-3 = all my jobs
man-cupsenable = enables a queue: it prints its jobs again. cupsd disables a queue itself when a printer fails and ErrorPolicy is stop-printer (the default); the queue stays disabled, across restarts, until someone enables it. Administrators only (the lpadmin group).
    .synopsis = cupsenable [-c] destination…
    .option-c = cancel the queue's jobs.
    .option-r = a reason (cupsdisable).
    .example-1 = enable the office queue
    .example-2 = check it
man-cupsdisable = disables a queue: it keeps accepting jobs but doesn't print them. -r gives a reason, shown by lpstat -p. Administrators only.
    .synopsis = cupsdisable [-c] [-r reason] destination…
    .option-c = cancel the queue's jobs.
    .option-r = the reason shown.
    .example-1 = stop the office queue, with a reason
    .example-2 = stop it and cancel its jobs
man-cupsaccept = lets a queue accept jobs again (cupsreject refuses them). Accepting and printing are two things: a queue can accept jobs while disabled, and the other way round. Administrators only.
    .synopsis = cupsaccept destination…
    .option-r = a reason (cupsreject).
    .example-1 = accept jobs on the office queue
    .example-2 = check which queues accept jobs
man-cupsreject = makes a queue refuse new jobs (the waiting ones still print if it is enabled). -r gives a reason. Administrators only.
    .synopsis = cupsreject [-r reason] destination…
    .option-r = the reason shown.
    .example-1 = refuse jobs until Monday
man-lpadmin = configures CUPS's queues. -p NAME creates or changes a queue: -v its device (ipp://, ipps://, socket://…), -m its driver (everywhere: IPP Everywhere, driverless, CUPS asks the printer; or a model of lpinfo -m), -P a PPD file, -D its description, -L its location, -o printer-is-shared=true|false, -u allow:users,@group (or deny:). A new queue is disabled and rejects jobs unless -E follows -p (before -p, -E means encryption). -d NAME sets the server's default destination, -x NAME deletes a queue. CUPS 2.4 warns that printer drivers (PPDs) are deprecated. Administrators only (the lpadmin group).
    .synopsis = lpadmin -p name [-E] [-v uri] [-m model] [-P ppd] [-D info] [-L location] [-o name=value] [-u allow:list|deny:list] ; lpadmin -d name ; lpadmin -x name
    .option-p = the queue to create or change.
    .option-E = after -p: enable the queue and accept jobs.
    .option-v = its device URI.
    .option-m = its model: everywhere, or a name from lpinfo -m.
    .option-P = a PPD file.
    .option-D = its description.
    .option-L = its location.
    .option-o = an option: printer-is-shared=true|false, printer-error-policy=…
    .option-u = the users allowed (allow:) or denied (deny:): names and @groups, or all.
    .option-d = the server's default destination.
    .option-x = delete a queue.
    .example-1 = a driverless queue for an IPP Everywhere printer, enabled
    .example-2 = make it the default destination
    .example-3 = only the staff group may print on it
    .example-4 = share it (or not: false)
    .example-5 = an old printer with a generic PPD
    .example-6 = delete a queue
man-lpoptions = shows and sets a user's printing options: -d sets YOUR default destination (in ~/.cups/lpoptions; root writes /etc/cups/lpoptions), not the server's (lpadmin -d). Without an option, the default queue's options; -l the options of its driver.
    .synopsis = lpoptions [-p destination] [-l] ; lpoptions -d destination ; lpoptions [-p destination] -o name=value
    .option-d = your default destination.
    .option-p = the destination.
    .option-l = the driver's options.
    .option-o = set an option.
    .option-x = remove the destination's options.
    .example-1 = the default queue's options
    .example-2 = what the office queue's driver offers
    .example-3 = make office your own default
man-lpinfo = lists what CUPS can use: -v the devices (the backends, and the printers found on the network: dnssd://, socket://…; administrators only), -m the drivers (models) installed, for lpadmin -m. --make-and-model filters the models.
    .synopsis = lpinfo -v [-l] ; lpinfo -m [-l] [--make-and-model text]
    .option-v = the devices.
    .option-m = the models.
    .option-l = long listing.
    .example-1 = the devices and printers found
    .example-2 = every driver installed
    .example-3 = the LaserJet drivers
    .example-4 = the drivers for a LaserJet 4250
man-cupsctl = shows or changes cupsd's main settings, in cupsd.conf, then restarts cupsd. Without an argument, the current settings. --[no-]share-printers (Port 631 and Allow @LOCAL in <Location />), --[no-]remote-admin (the same for /admin), --[no-]remote-any (Allow all instead of @LOCAL), --[no-]user-cancel-any (anyone may cancel any job), --[no-]debug-logging (LogLevel debug or warn); NAME=value sets a directive (MaxLogSize=1m, PreserveJobHistory=1w, JobPrivateValues=default…). Administrators only.
    .synopsis = cupsctl [--[no-]share-printers] [--[no-]remote-admin] [--[no-]remote-any] [--[no-]user-cancel-any] [--[no-]debug-logging] [name=value…]
    .example-1 = the current settings
    .example-2 = share the queues with the local networks
    .example-3 = the admin pages from this machine only
    .example-4 = back to LogLevel warn
    .example-5 = rotate the logs at 1 MB
    .example-6 = keep a week of history, no documents
man-cupsd = the CUPS scheduler (service cups): it reads /etc/cups/cupsd.conf and printers.conf when it starts, manages the queues and the jobs, the spool /var/spool/cups and the logs /var/log/cups (error_log, access_log, page_log). -t tests the configuration file and says what is wrong; run it before a restart. Its port is 631 (IPP and its web pages).
    .synopsis = cupsd -t ; systemctl status|restart cups
    .option-t = test the configuration file.
    .option-f = run in the foreground.
    .option-l = run on demand (systemd).
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = test cupsd.conf
    .example-2 = is the scheduler running
    .example-3 = restart it (to apply cupsd.conf)
man-driverless = lists the printers of the network that print driverless (IPP Everywhere, AirPrint, Mopria), as URIs lpadmin -v takes; with a URI, prints the PPD made from the printer's answers (cups-filters).
    .synopsis = driverless [--std-ipp-uris] [uri]
    .example-1 = the driverless printers announced on the network
    .example-2 = the same, as ipp://host:631/… URIs
man-cupsd_conf = cupsd's configuration: /etc/cups/cupsd.conf, read when cupsd starts (cupsd -t to check it). LogLevel (none, error, warn, info, debug, debug2: what error_log records), MaxLogSize (rotation of the logs, 0 = never), PreserveJobHistory and PreserveJobFiles (how long finished jobs and their documents are kept: Yes, No, seconds or 1w, 1d…), ErrorPolicy (stop-printer, retry-job, abort-job), Port and Listen (where cupsd listens: localhost:631 for the machine only), Browsing; <Location /path> blocks decide who may connect to a path: Order allow,deny (refuse by default) then Allow (an address, a network 198.51.100.0/24, @LOCAL: every network the server has an interface on, @IF(name), all) and Deny; the longest Location wins (/printers/NAME before /printers before /). <Policy default> with its <Limit operations> blocks decides who may do what (Require user @OWNER @SYSTEM), and JobPrivateValues which job attributes only their owner and the administrators see.
    .synopsis = /etc/cups/cupsd.conf
    .example-1 = errors and warnings only
    .example-2 = rotate the logs at 1 MB
    .example-3 = forget jobs after a week
    .example-4 = don't keep printed documents
    .example-5 = listen on the network
    .example-6 = a block for one queue
    .example-7 = refuse by default
    .example-8 = allow the staff network
    .example-9 = end of the block
man-printers_conf = the queues of CUPS: /etc/cups/printers.conf. cupsd writes it (when a queue changes, and when it stops) and reads it when it starts: DO NOT EDIT IT WHILE CUPSD IS RUNNING, cupsd would write over it. The tools are lpadmin, cupsenable, cupsaccept. Each queue: DeviceURI, MakeModel, State (Idle, Stopped) and StateMessage, Accepting, Shared, AllowUser/DenyUser, ErrorPolicy; its PPD is /etc/cups/ppd/NAME.ppd.
    .synopsis = /etc/cups/printers.conf
    .example-1 = read it (root only)
    .example-2 = stop cupsd before editing it by hand
man-update-ca-certificates = updates the certificates the system trusts: the directory /etc/ssl/certs and the bundle /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt that curl, wget and openssl read. It takes the authorities listed in /etc/ca-certificates.conf (Mozilla's, from the ca-certificates package) and every file ending in .crt under /usr/local/share/ca-certificates: that is where to put your own authority's certificate (the certificate only, never its key). A file ending in .pem there is ignored. It says how many were added and removed. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = update-ca-certificates [--fresh] [--verbose]
    .example-1 = trust the federation's root authority on this machine
    .example-2 = rebuild /etc/ssl/certs from scratch
    .example-3 = check that the bundle now holds it
man-forgejo = the command line of Forgejo, the self-hosted git forge. The server itself runs as the forgejo service (forgejo web, see systemctl); the command administers it: forgejo admin user list [--admin] lists the accounts; admin user create --username NAME --email ADDRESS --password PASS [--admin] [--random-password]; admin user change-password --username NAME --password PASS (a token doesn't depend on the password: changing it revokes nothing); admin user must-change-password NAME…; admin user delete --username NAME (or --id N) refuses an account that still owns repositories, unless --purge, which deletes them and everything the account wrote (its comments then show as Ghost: to stop an account, block it from signing in instead, prohibit_login in the API); admin user generate-access-token --username NAME --token-name NAME --scopes SCOPES (all, or write:repository, read:user…). Forgejo refuses to run as root, and its configuration (/etc/forgejo/app.ini) is readable by root and the forgejo group only: run it as forgejo, with --config. Everything else (collaborators, protected branches, secrets, runs) goes through the HTTP API: man forgejo-api.
    .synopsis = forgejo [--config FILE] [--work-path DIR] admin user list|create|change-password|delete|must-change-password|generate-access-token [OPTIONS] ; forgejo --version
    .option-c = the configuration file, app.ini (--config).
    .option-w = Forgejo's working directory (--work-path).
    .option-v = the version (--version).
    .example-1 = the forge's accounts
    .example-2 = give an account a new password
    .example-3 = delete an account (refused if it owns repositories)
    .example-4 = a token for a script, with only the scope it needs
    .example-5 = which Forgejo runs here
man-forgejo-runner = Forgejo's Actions runner, which runs the forge's workflows. It takes the jobs of the forge's workflows (.forgejo/workflows/*.yml in a repository) and runs them, here in containers (label docker). It runs as the forgejo-runner service (forgejo-runner daemon --config /etc/forgejo-runner/config.yml); its registration is in .runner, its labels in the configuration; journalctl -u forgejo-runner shows the tasks it took. A workflow says when it runs (on: push, pull_request, pull_request_target, workflow_dispatch…), its jobs (runs-on: a label, needs:, if: an expression), their steps (uses: an action such as actions/checkout@v4, or run: shell lines), and env: variables, from secrets with ${"{"}{"{"} secrets.NAME {"}"}{"}"}. A pull request from a fork gets no secrets on pull_request; pull_request_target runs the base branch's workflow with the secrets: never with the fork's code checked out. The runner masks secrets in the logs, only their exact text (base64 escapes it).
    .synopsis = forgejo-runner [--config FILE] daemon|generate-config|register|version
    .option-c = the configuration file (--config).
    .option-v = the version (--version).
    .example-1 = the runner's version
    .example-2 = is the runner running?
    .example-3 = the tasks it took
    .example-4 = its configuration, its labels
man-forgejo-api = Forgejo's HTTP API: accounts, tokens, repositories, protected branches, secrets, Actions runs. It lives under https://FORGE/api/v1 (here forge.lendshed.example; the swagger at /api/swagger). Authentication: a token (-H "Authorization: token TOKEN", its scopes decide: write:repository, write:admin…), or basic authentication (curl -u account:password), required to list or delete tokens. An administrator may act as another account with the header Sudo: NAME. Bodies are JSON (-H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '…'; jq -Rs builds a JSON string from a file). Answers are JSON, unindented: pipe them into jq. Routes: GET /user; GET /admin/users, PATCH /admin/users/NAME ({"{"}"prohibit_login": true{"}"} blocks an account and its tokens), DELETE /admin/users/NAME[?purge=true]; GET /users/NAME/tokens, DELETE /users/NAME/tokens/ID; GET /repos/OWNER/REPO, PATCH it ("private"); GET /repos/OWNER/REPO/collaborators, GET …/collaborators/NAME/permission, PUT or DELETE …/collaborators/NAME; GET or POST /repos/OWNER/REPO/branch_protections, GET, PATCH or DELETE …/branch_protections/RULE (rule_name, enable_push, enable_push_whitelist, push_whitelist_usernames, required_approvals, enable_status_check, status_check_contexts, require_signed_commits…; a protected branch always refuses a force-push); GET …/commits/REF/statuses (the CI's contexts: "workflow / job (event)"); GET …/actions/secrets, PUT or DELETE …/actions/secrets/NAME ({"{"}"data": "…"{"}"}; a secret's value is never shown again); GET …/actions/runs (each run, its jobs and their logs_url); GET …/pulls, …/forks, …/tags, …/hooks. Web pages: /OWNER/REPO/actions/runs/N/jobs/J/logs (a job's log, public for a public repository), /NAME.keys (an account's public SSH keys).
    .synopsis = curl [-s] [-u ACCOUNT:PASSWORD | -H "Authorization: token TOKEN"] [-X METHOD] [-H "Content-Type: application/json" -d JSON] https://FORGE/api/v1/ROUTE
    .example-1 = who am I, for this token
    .example-2 = the repository's direct collaborators
    .example-3 = another account's tokens, as an administrator (basic authentication)
    .example-4 = require signed commits on main
    .example-5 = replace a secret with the content of a file
    .example-6 = the Actions runs
    .example-7 = a job's log
    .example-8 = an account's public SSH keys
man-virsh = the command line of libvirt, to manage virtual machines (domains) and what they use: here, KVM guests of the local hypervisor, qemu:///system. virsh SUBCOMMAND [ARGUMENTS]; a domain is named by its name, its id (while it runs) or its uuid. Domains: list (the running ones; --all: the stopped ones too; --name), start, shutdown (presses the guest's ACPI power button: the guest must hear it and power off by itself; a paused guest hears nothing), destroy (pulls the plug: a hard stop, the last resort), reboot (the guest reboots inside the same QEMU process: its running definition doesn't change), suspend and resume (pause and wake), autostart [--disable] (start the domain with the host), dominfo, domstate [--reason] (running (booted), paused (I/O error)…), domblklist [--details], domiflist, domifaddr [--source lease|arp] (lease: what libvirt's own DHCP gave, on its networks only), domblkinfo, domblkerror (a disk's error: no space), dommemstat (the guest's memory: actual, unused, disk_caches, rss), domstats, vcpucount, console (the guest's serial console; Ctrl-] leaves it). Definitions: dumpxml [--inactive] (the running definition, or the next one), edit (opens the persistent definition in the editor, checks it against libvirt's schema, defines it; it applies at the next start), define FILE, undefine [--snapshots-metadata] [--remove-all-storage] (removes the definition; with --remove-all-storage, the disk images too). Resources: setmem SIZE, setvcpus N (--live: the running guest, through the balloon or hotplug; --config: the definition, for the next start; --current: whichever applies now), setmaxmem SIZE --config (the maximum, only for the next start). Sizes are scaled integers: KiB without a unit, or K, M, G (KiB, MiB, GiB). Disks: blockcommit DOMAIN DISK [--top IMAGE] [--base IMAGE] [--active] [--pivot] [--verbose] [--delete] (merges images of a backing chain down into a base while the guest runs; --active commits the top layer, --pivot then makes the base the disk), blockjob DOMAIN DISK [--pivot|--abort|--info], blockresize DOMAIN DISK SIZE (grows a running guest's disk; the guest sees it at once). Snapshots: snapshot-create-as DOMAIN NAME [DESCRIPTION] [--disk-only] (internal by default: inside the qcow2 image, with the memory if it runs; --disk-only: external, a new overlay file on top of the disk), snapshot-list [--tree] [--parent], snapshot-info, snapshot-current, snapshot-revert, snapshot-delete DOMAIN NAME [--metadata] (an external snapshot's overlay is merged into the image below it, then removed; --metadata only forgets the snapshot). Storage: pool-list, pool-info, vol-list POOL [--details]. Networks: net-list [--all], net-info, net-dumpxml, net-dhcp-leases NETWORK; attach-interface DOMAIN --type bridge|network --source NAME [--mac MAC] [--live] [--config], detach-interface DOMAIN --type TYPE [--mac MAC]. Host: nodeinfo, nodememstats, version, uri. help [SUBCOMMAND] lists the subcommands.
    .synopsis = virsh [-c URI] SUBCOMMAND [DOMAIN] [ARGUMENTS] [--OPTION[=VALUE]]…
    .option-c = the connection URI (--connect): here only qemu:///system.
    .option-q = quiet (--quiet).
    .option-r = read-only connection (--readonly).
    .option-h = the help (--help).
    .option-v = the version (--version).
    .example-1 = every domain, running or not
    .example-2 = a domain's details: its memory, its CPUs, whether it starts with the host
    .example-3 = why a domain is in its state
    .example-4 = a domain's disks, and their files
    .example-5 = a domain's interfaces: type, network or bridge, MAC
    .example-6 = start it with the host
    .example-7 = its persistent definition, in the editor
    .example-8 = merge a disk's whole backing chain into its base, and switch to it
    .example-9 = forget an external snapshot whose files are no longer used
    .example-10 = the guest's memory, as its balloon driver sees it
    .example-11 = less memory, now and at the next start
    .example-12 = grow a running guest's disk
    .example-13 = a safety net before a risky change, and its removal
    .example-14 = what libvirt's DHCP gave on the NAT network
    .example-15 = the addresses a domain's MACs have in the host's ARP table
man-qemu-img = QEMU's disk image tool: creates, inspects, converts, checks and resizes the images virtual machines use (qcow2: a copy-on-write format that only takes on the host what was written, can rest on a backing file, and can hold internal snapshots; raw: a plain file). An image a running QEMU uses is locked: qemu-img refuses to open it ("Failed to get shared "write" lock"); -U (--force-share) reads it anyway, never write to it. info [--backing-chain] [-U] IMAGE: its format, its virtual size (what the guest sees) and its disk size (what it takes on the host), its backing file, its internal snapshots. create -f FORMAT [-b BACKING -F FORMAT] IMAGE [SIZE]: a new image, empty or resting on another. resize [--shrink] IMAGE [+|-]SIZE: changes the virtual size (only while no QEMU uses it; to shrink, the guest's file system and partition must be shrunk first, and --shrink is required). convert -O FORMAT SOURCE DESTINATION: a copy, its chain flattened. commit IMAGE: merges an overlay into its backing file (the guest stopped). check IMAGE: the image's consistency. snapshot -l|-c NAME|-d NAME IMAGE: its internal snapshots. Sizes: k, M, G, T (powers of 1024).
    .synopsis = qemu-img info|create|resize|convert|commit|check|snapshot [OPTIONS] IMAGE [SIZE]
    .example-1 = an image and its whole chain, even while a guest uses it
    .example-2 = a new thin image of 20 GiB
    .example-3 = an overlay resting on a base image
    .example-4 = grow an image by 20 GiB (its guest stopped)
    .example-5 = a single, flattened copy of a chain
    .example-6 = check an image
    .example-7 = the internal snapshots of an image
man-virt-install = creates a new virtual machine and starts its installation: its name, its memory (MiB), its vCPUs, its disk (path=FILE,size=GiB creates the image), its network (network=default: libvirt's NAT network; bridge=br0: the host's bridge to the local network), and how it installs (--location, --cdrom, --import of an existing disk, --pxe). It defines the domain and starts it; --noautoconsole doesn't open its console.
    .synopsis = virt-install --name NAME --memory MiB [--vcpus N] --disk path=FILE[,size=GiB] [--network network=NAME|bridge=NAME] --import|--cdrom FILE|--location URL [--os-variant OS] [--noautoconsole]
    .example-1 = a guest from an existing disk, on the office's network
    .example-2 = a guest with a new 20 GiB disk, on the NAT network
man-virt-clone = clones a virtual machine: a new domain with a new name, a new uuid, new MAC addresses, and a copy of its disks. The source must be shut off (or paused). It doesn't touch what is inside the disks: the clone keeps the original's /etc/machine-id, host name and SSH host keys until they are reset (virt-sysprep, or by hand).
    .synopsis = virt-clone --original NAME --name NEW [--auto-clone | --file PATH]
    .example-1 = a clone of agenda, its disk named after it
    .example-2 = a clone whose disk goes to a chosen file
man-growpart = grows a partition so that it takes all the free space after it on its disk (cloud-guest-utils): after a virtual disk was made bigger, the partition must follow, then the file system (resize2fs for ext4). It rewrites the partition table of a disk in use safely; -N only shows what it would do.
    .synopsis = growpart [-N] DISK PARTITION
    .option-N = dry run: only show the change (--dry-run).
    .example-1 = what would change
    .example-2 = grow partition 1 of /dev/vda to the end of the disk
man-systemd-machine-id-setup = initialises /etc/machine-id, the machine's unique identity (32 hexadecimal digits), when it is missing or empty: in a KVM guest from the virtual machine's UUID, otherwise from a random number. An existing valid identity is left alone: to give a cloned machine its own, empty or remove /etc/machine-id first. Many programs derive their identity from it: systemd-networkd's DHCP client identifier, journald's files, D-Bus.
    .synopsis = systemd-machine-id-setup [--print] [--commit]
    .example-1 = a new identity for a clone
    .example-2 = the identity, as hostnamectl shows it
man-keepalived = the VRRP daemon (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol): several machines share a virtual address, held by one of them, the master; the others (backups) take it over when the master stops sending its advertisements. It reads /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf (man keepalived.conf) when it starts, and again on SIGHUP (systemctl reload keepalived); it runs as a service (keepalived.service) and writes to the journal: the state of each instance ("(VI_1) Entering MASTER STATE", "Entering BACKUP STATE", "Entering FAULT STATE"), its checks ("VRRP_Script(chk_haproxy) failed"), the priorities it hears, and the packets it drops ("bogus VRRP packet received"). -t (--config-test) checks the configuration and exits: nothing printed means no error found; -f (--use-file) reads another file; -v (--version) shows its version.
    .synopsis = keepalived -t [-f FILE] ; keepalived -v ; systemctl start|stop|reload keepalived
    .option-t = check the configuration file and exit (--config-test).
    .option-f = use this configuration file (--use-file).
    .option-v = the version (--version).
    .example-1 = check /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf
    .example-2 = check another file
    .example-3 = read the configuration again (SIGHUP): an instance that kept its virtual_router_id keeps its state
    .example-4 = what keepalived did lately
    .example-5 = its version
man-keepalived_conf = keepalived's configuration, /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf: blocks between braces, one keyword per line, comments after # or !. global_defs: router_id NAME; enable_script_security and script_user USER (who runs the check scripts). vrrp_script NAME: a check run every `interval` seconds, script "COMMAND" (exit code 0: success), weight N (-253 to 253: added to the priority while the check fails if negative, while it succeeds if positive; 0, the default: a failure puts the instance in FAULT state, it gives the address up and stops advertising), fall N and rise N (how many results in a row change its state). vrrp_instance NAME: state MASTER|BACKUP (only the state it starts in: the election decides), interface (where it advertises), virtual_router_id 1-255 (the VRRP group: the same on every node of the group, and unique on the link), priority 1-255 (the highest wins; on a tie, the highest address), advert_int (seconds between two advertisements; a backup that hears none for three of them, plus a little, takes over), nopreempt (with state BACKUP: a backup with a higher priority doesn't take the address from a master that still advertises), authentication (auth_type PASS, auth_pass: avoid it, VRRPv3 dropped it), virtual_ipaddress (the shared addresses), track_script (the vrrp_script names whose results change this instance's priority, optionally with their own weight), unicast_src_ip and unicast_peer (send the advertisements to these addresses instead of the 224.0.0.18 group). The advertisements are IP protocol 112 (vrrp): a firewall between the nodes must let them in.
    .synopsis = /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf
    .example-1 = a check of haproxy: a failure takes 20 from the priority
    .example-2 = inside a vrrp_instance: follow that check
    .example-3 = advertise to the peer only (each node lists the other)
man-haproxy = a load balancer and reverse proxy (TCP and HTTP): it receives the clients' connections on a frontend (bind) and sends them to the servers of a backend (balance roundrobin: in turn; leastconn: the one with the fewest connections; first). It reads /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg (sections global, defaults, frontend, backend, listen) when it starts or reloads; systemctl reload haproxy checks the file first and keeps the old process alive for its open connections. A server line: server NAME ADDRESS:PORT [check] [inter TIME] [fall N] [rise N] [backup] [weight N] [disabled]. Without check, HAProxy never checks it ("no check"); with check alone, it only opens a TCP connection (L4OK); with option httpchk [METHOD] URI in the backend, it sends an HTTP request (L7OK, or L7STS for a wrong status), and http-check expect status CODE says which status is good (by default 2xx and 3xx). A server is marked DOWN after `fall` failed checks in a row (3 by default) and UP again after `rise` good ones (2), checks every `inter` (2s). The runtime API, on the stats socket of the global section (stats socket /run/haproxy/admin.sock), changes the running process without touching the file (lost at the next reload): show stat (CSV: pxname, svname, scur the current sessions, status, check_status…), show servers state, show info, set server BACKEND/SERVER state ready|drain|maint (drain: no new connections, the open ones finish; maint: no traffic at all and no checks), enable server / disable server, set weight. Its log goes through syslog to /var/log/haproxy.log on Debian (the HTTP log format: client, date, frontend, backend/server, timers, status, bytes, termination state, "request"). -c checks the configuration (-f FILE): "Configuration file is valid", or ALERT lines with the file and line number.
    .synopsis = haproxy -c -f FILE [-q] ; haproxy -v|-vv ; systemctl reload haproxy
    .option-c = check the configuration and exit.
    .option-f = the configuration file (can be repeated).
    .option-q = quiet: print nothing if the configuration is valid.
    .option-v = the version (-vv: and how it was built).
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = apply it (the file is checked first)
    .example-3 = each server's sessions, state and last check
    .example-4 = take a server out for maintenance: the open sessions finish
    .example-5 = put it back
    .example-6 = the servers' runtime state
    .example-7 = follow the requests and the checks
man-socat = relays data between two addresses: here the standard input and output (STDIO, or -) and a UNIX socket (a path, or UNIX-CONNECT:PATH). The usual way to talk to a daemon's control socket from a script: what socat reads on its input goes to the socket, and the answer comes back on its output. Connecting needs read and write rights on the socket.
    .synopsis = socat [OPTIONS] ADDRESS1 ADDRESS2
    .example-1 = HAProxy's process information
    .example-2 = the same with the explicit address types
man-tcpdump = captures and shows the packets that reach (or leave) a network interface, before the firewall looks at them: what tcpdump sees arrived on the wire, whatever the firewall then decides. -i the interface, -c stop after N packets, -n don't turn addresses into names, -v more detail (for VRRP: the virtual addresses), -e the MAC addresses. The expression (pcap-filter) chooses the packets: vrrp (IP protocol 112, the advertisements of keepalived and routers), arp, host ADDRESS, src ADDRESS, dst ADDRESS, combined with and, or, not. A VRRP line reads: source > 224.0.0.18 (vrrp.mcast.net): VRRPv2, Advertisement, vrid (the group), prio (the priority), authtype, intvl (the interval). Capturing needs root.
    .synopsis = tcpdump [-env] [-c COUNT] [-i INTERFACE] [EXPRESSION]
    .option-i = the interface to listen on (--interface).
    .option-c = stop after this many packets.
    .option-n = show addresses, not names.
    .option-v = more detail (-vv: even more).
    .option-e = show the link-level (MAC) addresses.
    .example-1 = the VRRP advertisements on eth0
    .example-2 = five of them, with addresses
    .example-3 = the same in detail (the virtual addresses)
    .example-4 = who asks and who answers for an address (ARP)
    .example-5 = only those of one node
man-pidof = prints the PIDs of the running programs with this exact name (the newest first); exit code 1 if there is none: a common health check in scripts (pidof haproxy). -s only one PID.
    .synopsis = pidof [-s] PROGRAM...
    .option-s = a single PID.
    .example-1 = haproxy's processes
    .example-2 = one of keepalived's
man-doveadm = administers Dovecot and the mailboxes it stores, as root: doveadm COMMAND [-u USER | -A] [ARGUMENTS]. quota get shows a user's quota (Value and Limit in kilobytes, the percentage; MESSAGE counts the messages) and quota recalc counts the usage again from the mailboxes (needed when files were removed behind Dovecot's back). mailbox list, create NAME, delete NAME, rename OLD NEW, status "FIELDS" NAME (fields: messages, vsize, unseen, recent, guid…; "*" for every mailbox). search QUERY lists the matching messages (mailbox guid and uid), fetch "FIELDS" QUERY shows them (mailbox, uid, date.received, date.saved, size.virtual, hdr.subject, hdr, body, text), expunge [-d] QUERY removes them for good through Dovecot, which keeps its indexes and the quota right (the queries: man doveadm-search-query). user USER shows what the userdb returns for a user (uid, gid, home, and the extra fields such as quota_storage_size); a mask with * lists the users. sieve list|get|put [-a]|activate|deactivate|delete|rename manages a user's Sieve scripts in its personal storage (put reads the script from standard input and compiles it first; -a also activates it). log errors shows Dovecot's last errors; reload makes the running Dovecot read its configuration again. A user that doesn't exist: "User doesn't exist" (exit 67).
    .synopsis = doveadm [-Dv] [-f FORMATTER] COMMAND [-u USER|-A] [ARGUMENTS]
    .option-D = debug messages.
    .option-v = verbose.
    .option-o = override a setting: -o name=value.
    .option-c = read another configuration file.
    .option-f = the output's formatter: table, tab, pager, flow.
    .option-u = the user (or a mask with * and ?).
    .option-A = every user of the userdb.
    .example-1 = a user's quota
    .example-2 = count the usage again from the files
    .example-3 = the size of each of her mailboxes
    .example-4 = which messages a query matches, before removing them
    .example-5 = look at them: mailbox, save date, subject
    .example-6 = expunge what was saved in the Trash more than a year ago
    .example-7 = what the userdb says about her (home, quota…)
    .example-8 = create a mailbox
    .example-9 = a user's Sieve scripts, and which one is active
    .example-10 = store a script, compiled, and make it the active one
    .example-11 = activate a stored script
    .example-12 = Dovecot's last errors
    .example-13 = the running Dovecot reads its configuration again
man-doveadm-search-query = the search queries that doveadm's mail commands (search, fetch, expunge) take, close to IMAP SEARCH: keys joined by AND, OR between two keys (in parentheses when mixed with AND, escaped from the shell), NOT before a key. mailbox NAME (wildcards * and %) limits the query to mailboxes; ALL matches everything. Dates: before, since, on (the received date, IMAP's internal date), savedbefore, savedsince, savedon (when the message was saved in this mailbox), sentbefore, sentsince (the Date: header); a date is YYYY-MM-DD, dd-Mon-yyyy, a Unix timestamp, or an interval back from now: a number and a unit, weeks (w), days (d), hours (h), mins (m), secs (s), so 2w or 30days (there is no year unit). Sizes: larger and smaller, in bytes or with k, M, G. Text: from, to, cc, subject, body, text (headers and body), header NAME STRING. Flags: seen, unseen, deleted, new, old.
    .synopsis = doveadm search|fetch|expunge -u USER [mailbox NAME] KEY [VALUE] [OR KEY VALUE] [NOT KEY VALUE]…
    .example-1 = what the Trash holds from before 2031
    .example-2 = the big messages of the last two weeks in INBOX
    .example-3 = an OR between two keys, parentheses escaped
man-doveconf = shows Dovecot's configuration as Dovecot reads it: dovecot.conf and everything it includes (conf.d/*.conf), without the comments. Without arguments (or -n), the settings that were set; with NAMES, only those settings or sections (a section inside another: service/quota-warning). A file Dovecot can't read stops it: "Error in configuration file FILE line N: Unknown setting: …". Reading the configuration doesn't apply it: the running Dovecot reads it again on doveadm reload (or systemctl reload dovecot).
    .synopsis = doveconf [-adnNPx] [-c FILE] [-h] [NAME…]
    .option-n = only the settings that were set (the default).
    .option-a = every setting.
    .option-d = the default values.
    .option-h = the value only, without the setting's name.
    .option-x = expand variables and included files' contents.
    .option-P = show the passwords too.
    .example-1 = the configuration Dovecot reads
    .example-2 = the quota section
    .example-3 = one setting's value
    .example-4 = one service
    .example-5 = the quota warnings, with their lines
man-sievec = compiles a Sieve script (Pigeonhole, Dovecot's Sieve): checks its syntax, its require and its commands, and writes the compiled binary next to it (NAME.svbin), or to OUT-FILE. An error stops it with the script's line ("NAME: line 3: error: unknown command 'filein' (only reported once at first occurrence)", then "error: validation failed" or "error: parse failed") and exit code 1. A directory compiles each .sieve file in it. Dovecot compiles scripts on its own at delivery; sievec is what tells you a script is wrong before it is.
    .synopsis = sievec [-c CONFIG] [-d] [-D] [-o SETTING=VALUE] [-u USER] SCRIPT-FILE [OUT-FILE]
    .option-c = another Dovecot configuration file.
    .option-d = write a dump of the compiled script instead of the binary (to stdout without OUT-FILE).
    .option-D = Sieve debugging.
    .option-o = override a setting.
    .option-u = the user the script is compiled for.
    .example-1 = check and compile a script
    .example-2 = compile a user's script in place
    .example-3 = what the compiled script contains
man-sieve-test = runs a Sieve script on a message stored in a file and prints what would be done (the performed actions: store message in folder, send vacation message, redirect, reject; then the implicit keep), without storing or sending anything. The envelope: -f the sender (otherwise Return-Path:, Sender:, From:), -a the original recipient (an alias), -r the final recipient (the script's owner, whom vacation answers for); otherwise the To: header. -s runs another script first (a global before script). Vacation's checks that depend on what was sent before (one reply per sender) and on the real delivery are made at delivery: watch mail.log for the real result.
    .synopsis = sieve-test [-a ORIG-RECIPIENT] [-r RECIPIENT] [-f SENDER] [-m MAILBOX] [-s SCRIPT]… [-t TRACE-FILE] [-C] [-D] [-e] SCRIPT-FILE MAIL-FILE
    .option-a = the original envelope recipient.
    .option-r = the final envelope recipient.
    .option-f = the envelope sender.
    .option-m = the mailbox the implicit keep stores into (INBOX).
    .option-s = a script to run before the main one.
    .option-t = a trace file (- for the screen).
    .option-C = compile again, ignoring an existing binary.
    .option-D = Sieve debugging.
    .option-e = execute the actions for real (needs the storage settings).
    .example-1 = what a script does with a sample
    .example-2 = as delivered to Aziz through treasurer@
    .example-3 = the global script first, then the user's
man-rspamc = rspamd's client: sends messages to the running rspamd (localhost:11333, its controller on 11334) and prints its answer. rspamc [symbols] FILE… (the default command; from standard input without files) shows each message's action (no action, greylist, add header, rewrite subject, reject), whether it is spam, its score against the reject threshold and each symbol with its score and its options. stat shows the counters: messages scanned, by action, learned, and the Bayes statfiles (learned: how many messages each class has). learn_spam and learn_ham FILE|DIRECTORY… teach Bayes a message's class (a directory: every file under it); a message already learned in that class: "has been already learned as spam, ignore it" (HTTP 208); learned in the other class, it is learned again. Bayes only classifies once each class has min_learns messages. -i, -F, -r give the SMTP client's address, sender and recipient, as the milter would.
    .synopsis = rspamc [-h HOST] [-P PASSWORD] [-i IP] [-F FROM] [-r RCPT] [symbols|stat|learn_spam|learn_ham] [FILE|DIRECTORY…]
    .option-h = rspamd's address (localhost:11333).
    .option-P = the controller's password.
    .option-i = the SMTP client's IP address.
    .option-F = the SMTP sender (MAIL FROM).
    .option-r = an SMTP recipient (RCPT TO).
    .option-d = the user the message is delivered to (per-user statistics).
    .example-1 = a message's score, action and symbols
    .example-2 = the counters, and what Bayes learned
    .example-3 = teach Bayes the spam already sorted
    .example-4 = and the mail people kept
    .example-5 = as if it came from the platform's server
man-rspamadm = rspamd's administration tool. configtest reads the configuration as rspamd would (rspamd.conf, then local.d/ merged with the defaults and override.d/ replacing them) and says "syntax OK", or the error and the file's line, with exit code 1: run it before systemctl reload rspamd. configdump [SECTION…] prints the configuration as rspamd understands it (actions, multimap, settings).
    .synopsis = rspamadm configtest | rspamadm configdump [SECTION…]
    .example-1 = check the configuration before a reload
    .example-2 = the thresholds rspamd will use
    .example-3 = the local maps and settings
man-upsc = shows what upsd knows about a UPS (Network UPS Tools). upsc -l lists the UPS names a server serves (the sections of its ups.conf), -L with their description; upsc UPS lists all its variables (battery.charge in %, battery.runtime in seconds, battery.mfr.date, ups.status: OL on line, OB on battery, LB low battery, CHRG/DISCHRG, RB replace battery, CAL calibrating, FSD forced shutdown; ups.load in %, input.voltage, ups.test.result, ups.delay.shutdown…), upsc UPS VARIABLE only one; upsc -c UPS lists the clients logged in (each upsmon). A UPS is written NAME[@HOST[:PORT]] (localhost and 3493 by default). Needs no account. Errors: "Error: Unknown UPS", "Error: Driver not connected", "Error: Connection failure: Connection refused".
    .synopsis = upsc -l | -L [HOST[:PORT]] ; upsc UPS[@HOST[:PORT]] [VARIABLE] ; upsc -c UPS
    .option-l = list the UPS names of a server.
    .option-L = the same, with each UPS's description.
    .option-c = list the clients logged into a UPS.
    .option-V = the version.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = the UPSes the local upsd serves
    .example-2 = everything the UPS says
    .example-3 = seconds of battery left at the present load
    .example-4 = its status, asked to another machine's upsd
    .example-5 = who is logged in (each upsmon)
man-upscmd = sends an instant command to a UPS through upsd: a battery test (test.battery.start.quick: a few seconds; test.battery.start.deep: a calibration that drains the battery), test.battery.stop, beeper.mute, shutdown.return… upscmd -l UPS lists what this UPS supports. The account (-u, -p: asked when missing) must be allowed the command in upsd.users (instcmds = the command, or ALL). Answers OK; otherwise "Instant command failed: Access denied", "Unknown UPS", "Instant command not supported". The result of a test is in upsc's ups.test.result.
    .synopsis = upscmd -l UPS ; upscmd [-u USER] [-p PASSWORD] UPS COMMAND [VALUE]
    .option-l = list the UPS's instant commands.
    .option-u = the upsd.users account.
    .option-p = its password (asked when missing; on the command line it stays in the history).
    .option-w = wait for the driver's result.
    .option-t = with -w, how long to wait (seconds).
    .option-V = the version.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = what this UPS can do
    .example-2 = a quick battery test (the password is asked)
    .example-3 = stop a test in progress
man-upsrw = changes a UPS's writable variables through upsd. Without -s (or with -l), lists them with their description and value (battery.charge.low, battery.runtime.low: when the UPS says LB; battery.mfr.date: the battery's date, to write when you change it; input.transfer.low/high; ups.delay.shutdown, ups.delay.start). -s VARIABLE=VALUE sets one; the account needs actions = SET in upsd.users. Answers OK; otherwise "Set variable failed: Access denied", "Read-only variable", "Invalid value for variable".
    .synopsis = upsrw [-l] UPS ; upsrw -s VARIABLE[=VALUE] [-u USER] [-p PASSWORD] UPS
    .option-s = the variable to set (VARIABLE=VALUE, or the value is asked).
    .option-l = list the writable variables.
    .option-u = the upsd.users account.
    .option-p = its password.
    .option-w = wait for the driver's result.
    .option-t = with -w, how long to wait (seconds).
    .option-V = the version.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = the writable variables and their values
    .example-2 = write the new battery's date
man-upsmon = the UPS monitor (service nut-monitor): it reads /etc/nut/upsmon.conf, logs into upsd for each MONITOR line (as a primary: the machine wired to the UPS, which may set FSD; or a secondary), polls the UPS every POLLFREQ seconds and notifies each change (ONBATT, ONLINE, LOWBATT, FSD, COMMBAD, REPLBATT…: syslog, wall, NOTIFYCMD). A UPS is critical when it is on battery AND low battery, or in FSD; when fewer than MINSUPPLIES supplies are healthy, upsmon shuts down: a primary sets FSD, waits up to HOSTSYNC seconds for the secondaries to log out, then (after FINALDELAY) creates POWERDOWNFLAG and runs SHUTDOWNCMD; at the end of the halt, the shutdown scripts see the flag (upsmon -K) and tell the UPS to cut its output (upsdrvctl shutdown), which comes back with the power. -c sends a command to the running upsmon: reload (reread upsmon.conf), stop, fsd (a REAL forced shutdown: NUT's way to test the whole sequence, when nobody needs the machines). A UPS upsmon never heard from is never critical: check that it is logged in (upsc -c UPS) and that its journal has no "Poll UPS … failed".
    .synopsis = upsmon -c fsd|reload|stop ; upsmon -K ; systemctl status|reload nut-monitor
    .option-c = send a command to the running upsmon: fsd, reload or stop.
    .option-K = is the power down flag set (exit code 0 if so).
    .option-P = the PID to signal (instead of the PID file).
    .option-D = more debugging (and stay in the foreground).
    .option-F = stay in the foreground (the service uses it).
    .option-u = the user the unprivileged child runs as.
    .option-V = the version.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = is upsmon running, and its last lines
    .example-2 = reread upsmon.conf
    .example-3 = a forced shutdown: the real drill (it halts the machine!)
    .example-4 = was the power down flag left set
man-upsd = the UPS information server (service nut-server): it serves the UPSes of /etc/nut/ups.conf (their drivers' data) to upsc, upscmd, upsrw and each upsmon, on port 3493. It reads upsd.conf (LISTEN: where it listens; without any, 127.0.0.1 and ::1 only; read ONLY at start: restart, don't reload) and upsd.users (the accounts and what they may do: reread on reload). Its journal says where it listens ("listening on …", "not listening on …") and who logs in.
    .synopsis = upsd -c reload|stop ; systemctl status|reload|restart nut-server
    .option-c = send a command to the running upsd: reload or stop.
    .option-P = the PID to signal.
    .option-D = more debugging.
    .option-F = stay in the foreground (the service uses it).
    .option-u = the user it switches to.
    .option-V = the version.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = is upsd running, and its last lines
    .example-2 = reread upsd.users and ups.conf
    .example-3 = restart it (needed after a LISTEN change)
man-upsdrvctl = starts and stops the UPS drivers of /etc/nut/ups.conf (on Debian, each one is a service: nut-driver@NAME). upsdrvctl shutdown sends the UPS its kill power command: it cuts its outlets offdelay seconds later and gives them back ondelay seconds after the command if the power is there; the shutdown scripts run it at the very end of an automatic shutdown. Typed by hand, it cuts whatever runs on the UPS. -t only prints what it would do: NUT's first step to test a shutdown.
    .synopsis = upsdrvctl [-t] start|stop|shutdown [UPS]
    .option-t = testing mode: print the actions without doing them.
    .option-u = the user the drivers switch to.
    .option-D = more debugging.
    .option-h = help.
    .example-1 = what a shutdown would send, without sending it
    .example-2 = the driver's service
    .example-3 = stop a driver
man-upssched = upsmon's timer helper: set NOTIFYCMD to upssched in upsmon.conf, and each notification (with EXEC in its NOTIFYFLAG) goes to upssched, which reads /etc/nut/upssched.conf: AT TYPE UPS START-TIMER name seconds, CANCEL-TIMER name [cmd], EXECUTE cmd. When a timer fires, upssched runs CMDSCRIPT with the timer's name. A timer started on ONBATT and cancelled on ONLINE tells a flicker from an outage. upssched needs CMDSCRIPT, PIPEFN and LOCKFN before its first AT ("must be set before any ATs in the config file!"). It isn't meant to be run by hand.
    .synopsis = NOTIFYCMD /usr/sbin/upssched (in upsmon.conf)
    .example-1 = in upsmon.conf: notifications go to upssched
    .example-2 = in upsmon.conf: ONBATT is logged, shown and passed on
man-ipmitool = talks to the machine's BMC (its management controller): chassis status (power, the power restore policy, the last power event), chassis policy list|always-on|previous|always-off (what the machine does when power comes back: always-on starts it, previous only if it was running when power went, always-off never), power status, sel list (the system event log: AC lost, power state changes). As root.
    .synopsis = ipmitool chassis status|policy POLICY|power status ; ipmitool sel list
    .example-1 = the chassis's state and its power restore policy
    .example-2 = the policies it supports
    .example-3 = start whenever power comes back
    .example-4 = the BMC's event log
man-ups_conf = the UPSes and their drivers: /etc/nut/ups.conf. One section per UPS, [name]: the name upsd, upsc and upsmon use. driver (usbhid-ups for USB UPSes), port (auto for USB), desc; for usbhid-ups, offdelay (seconds between the kill power command and the cut, 20 by default) and ondelay (seconds before the power comes back, 30 by default; must be greater than offdelay). A driver reads it when it starts (systemctl restart nut-driver@NAME); upsdrvctl shutdown reads it when it runs.
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/ups.conf
    .example-1 = a UPS called hallups
    .example-2 = its driver
    .example-3 = on USB
    .example-4 = cut 180 seconds after the kill power command
    .example-5 = power back after 240
man-upsd_conf = upsd's configuration: /etc/nut/upsd.conf. LISTEN ADDRESS [PORT] (several lines possible): where upsd waits for its clients; without any, 127.0.0.1 and ::1 only. Read only when upsd starts: restart nut-server after a change. Listen where the secondaries are, and let only them through the firewall.
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/upsd.conf
    .example-1 = the machine itself
    .example-2 = and the office network
man-upsd_users = upsd's accounts: /etc/nut/upsd.users, a section per account, [name]: password; actions = SET (change variables, upsrw) FSD; instcmds = a command (several lines possible) or ALL (upscmd); upsmon primary (the machine wired to the UPS: may log in and set FSD) or upsmon secondary (may log in and follow). Before NUT 2.8, primary was master and secondary slave (still accepted). Reread on reload.
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/upsd.users
    .example-1 = upsmon's account
    .example-2 = its password
    .example-3 = the primary's rights
    .example-4 = an administrator
    .example-5 = may change variables
    .example-6 = and run the quick test
man-upsmon_conf = upsmon's configuration: /etc/nut/upsmon.conf. MONITOR UPS@HOST POWERVALUE USER PASSWORD primary|secondary (the UPS as upsd names it); MINSUPPLIES (how many supplies must stay healthy: 1 for one power supply; 0 never shuts down); SHUTDOWNCMD (what halts the machine); NOTIFYCMD (a program run for the notifications flagged EXEC, with the message as $1 and NOTIFYTYPE, UPSNAME in the environment; or upssched); NOTIFYFLAG TYPE SYSLOG+WALL+EXEC (or IGNORE); NOTIFYMSG; POLLFREQ, POLLFREQALERT; HOSTSYNC (how long a primary waits for its secondaries to log out); DEADTIME; FINALDELAY; POWERDOWNFLAG (the file that tells the shutdown scripts to cut the UPS); RBWARNTIME; POLLFAIL_LOG_THROTTLE_MAX (how often failed polls are logged).
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/upsmon.conf
    .example-1 = the UPS wired to this machine
    .example-2 = a secondary following belay's UPS
    .example-3 = one supply must stay healthy
    .example-4 = how to halt
    .example-5 = notifications go to upssched
    .example-6 = ONBATT: logged, shown, executed
    .example-7 = wait 15 s for the secondaries
    .example-8 = 5 s between the warning and the halt
man-upssched_conf = upssched's configuration: /etc/nut/upssched.conf. CMDSCRIPT (the program run with a timer's name), PIPEFN and LOCKFN (its files, in a directory of the nut user): all three before the first AT. AT TYPE UPS START-TIMER name seconds; AT TYPE UPS CANCEL-TIMER name [cmd] (cmd runs if the timer had already fired); AT TYPE UPS EXECUTE cmd. UPS is NAME@HOST, or * for all.
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/upssched.conf
    .example-1 = the script run with the timer's name
    .example-2 = its pipe
    .example-3 = its lock
    .example-4 = wait 60 s on battery
    .example-5 = power back: cancel it (or say it's back if it had fired)
    .example-6 = a replace battery warning, at once
man-nut_conf = which NUT parts the machine runs: /etc/nut/nut.conf, MODE=none (nothing: Debian's default), standalone (driver, upsd and upsmon for this machine's UPS), netserver (the same, serving others over the network: LISTEN in upsd.conf), netclient (only upsmon, following a UPS served by another machine). No spaces around =.
    .synopsis = /etc/nut/nut.conf
    .example-1 = one machine, its UPS
    .example-2 = a UPS served to others
    .example-3 = a machine that follows another's UPS
man-garage = administers a Garage cluster, a geo-distributed object store with an S3 API (run as root: it reads the RPC secret in /etc/garage.toml). status lists the healthy nodes (ID, host, address, zone, capacity, the space left) and, below FAILED NODES, those that have a role but no longer answer. Buckets: bucket list, info NAME (size, objects, website access, the keys and their permissions RWO), create NAME, delete NAME --yes (empty buckets only), allow|deny --read --write --owner NAME --key KEY (read: list and get objects; write: put and delete them; owner: the bucket's settings, its website access, its deletion), website --allow|--deny NAME (website access: the web endpoint serves every object of the bucket to anyone, without a key; --index-document, --error-document). Keys: key list, create NAME (prints the secret once), info NAME [--show-secret], delete NAME --yes, allow|deny --create-bucket NAME. A key is named by its name or its ID (GK…). The layout (which node stores what): layout show (the current roles, the staged changes, the next version), layout assign -z ZONE -c CAPACITY [-t TAG] NODE [--replace OLD], layout remove NODE, layout revert --yes, layout apply --version N; a node is named by the beginning of its ID. With replication_factor = 3, each piece of data is on three nodes in different zones, and a read or a write needs two answers. stats and node id give the node's tables and identity.
    .synopsis = garage [-c FILE] status | bucket SUBCOMMAND | key SUBCOMMAND | layout SUBCOMMAND | node id | stats
    .option-c = the configuration file (/etc/garage.toml by default).
    .option-h = the node to talk to, NODE_ID@ADDRESS:PORT (the local one by default).
    .option-s = the RPC secret, instead of the configuration's rpc_secret.
    .option-V = the version.
    .example-1 = the nodes, healthy and failed
    .example-2 = the buckets
    .example-3 = a bucket: its size, its website access, its keys
    .example-4 = a new bucket (private: no key may use it yet)
    .example-5 = let a key read and write a bucket
    .example-6 = take write away from a key on a bucket
    .example-7 = serve a bucket as a website, to anyone
    .example-8 = the keys
    .example-9 = a new key: its ID and its secret, shown once
    .example-10 = a key, its secret and its buckets
    .example-11 = delete a key: whatever it signed stops working
    .example-12 = the layout, and the changes waiting to be applied
    .example-13 = give a new node a failed node's role
    .example-14 = apply the staged layout
man-aws = the AWS command-line tool (version 2), used here with an S3-compatible store. Its settings come from the command line (--profile, --region, --endpoint-url), then the environment (AWS_PROFILE, AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL), then the profile in ~/.aws/credentials (the key: aws_access_key_id, aws_secret_access_key) and ~/.aws/config ([default] or [profile NAME]: region, endpoint_url). aws s3: ls [s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]] (PRE: a common prefix, the "folders"), cp and mv (a local path, s3://BUCKET/KEY or - for the standard input or output; --recursive for a prefix), rm, sync SOURCE DEST (copies what is new or changed; --delete removes from DEST what isn't in SOURCE), mb and rb (make and remove a bucket), presign s3://BUCKET/KEY [--expires-in SECONDS] (a URL that lets whoever has it download that object until it expires: 3600 s by default, 604800 s at most; signed on this machine, with no request), website. --dryrun shows what would be done; --exclude and --include filter. aws s3api calls one operation and prints its JSON: list-buckets, list-objects-v2, head-object, get-object, delete-object, get-bucket-website, put-bucket-lifecycle-configuration, get-bucket-lifecycle-configuration. aws configure asks for the key, the secret, the region and the output format; configure set NAME VALUE, get NAME, list. Errors: "An error occurred (CODE) when calling the OPERATION operation: MESSAGE" (exit code 254, or 1 for a failed transfer); "Unable to locate credentials" (253).
    .synopsis = aws [--profile NAME] [--region REGION] [--endpoint-url URL] s3 ls|cp|mv|rm|sync|mb|rb|presign|website … ; aws s3api OPERATION --bucket NAME … ; aws configure [set|get|list]
    .option--profile = the profile to use, in ~/.aws/credentials and ~/.aws/config.
    .option--region = the region the requests are signed for.
    .option--endpoint-url = the S3 endpoint (for a store other than Amazon's).
    .option--no-sign-request = send the requests without a key (anonymously).
    .option--no-verify-ssl = don't check the endpoint's certificate (dangerous).
    .option--output = the output format: json, text, table.
    .option--recursive = cp, mv, rm, ls: every object under the prefix.
    .option--dryrun = show what would be done, do nothing.
    .option--delete = sync: also remove from the destination what the source no longer has.
    .option--exclude = leave out what matches this pattern (* and ?).
    .option--include = take back what matches this pattern, after an --exclude.
    .option--expires-in = presign: how long the URL works, in seconds.
    .example-1 = the buckets the key may use
    .example-2 = everything in a bucket, with readable sizes
    .example-3 = upload a file
    .example-4 = move a "folder" into another bucket
    .example-5 = what sync would upload and delete
    .example-6 = a download link for a week
    .example-7 = with another profile's key
    .example-8 = put a key in a profile
    .example-9 = where each setting comes from
    .example-10 = an object's size, ETag and date
    .example-11 = a lifecycle rule from a JSON file
man-rclone = copies and synchronises files between a local file system and remotes (here an S3 store), defined in its configuration file (~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf, or --config FILE, or RCLONE_CONFIG): a [NAME] section with type = s3, provider, access_key_id, secret_access_key, region, endpoint. A remote path is NAME:BUCKET/PATH. ls lists the files with their size, lsl with their date, lsd the buckets or folders, cat prints files, size adds them up. copy SOURCE DEST copies what is new or changed (it deletes nothing); copyto copies one file to one name; sync SOURCE DEST makes DEST the same as SOURCE: it copies what changed AND deletes from DEST what SOURCE doesn't have; mind the direction. --backup-dir NAME:PATH (on the same remote, not inside DEST) keeps what sync or copy replaces or deletes, moved there instead; -n (--dry-run) says what it would do and does nothing. check SOURCE DEST compares both sides file by file (size and MD5) and counts the differences (exit code 1 if there are any). rclone logs to the error output ("ERROR :", "NOTICE:"); a failed operation is tried three times ("Attempt 1/3 failed"), and nothing is deleted after an error. config show prints the remotes, listremotes their names.
    .synopsis = rclone [--config FILE] [-n] [-v] ls|lsl|lsd|cat|size|copy|copyto|sync|check|config show|listremotes [SOURCE] [DEST]
    .option-n = dry run: say what would be copied and deleted, do nothing (--dry-run).
    .option-v = say what is done (INFO), and the statistics at the end.
    .option-P = show the progress and the statistics.
    .option--config = the configuration file.
    .option--backup-dir = sync, copy: move what is replaced or deleted into this place of the same remote.
    .option--suffix = with --backup-dir: add this to the names kept.
    .option--no-check-certificate = don't check the server's certificate (dangerous).
    .example-1 = the remotes of the configuration
    .example-2 = their settings
    .example-3 = the files of a remote path
    .example-4 = the buckets
    .example-5 = what sync would copy and delete
    .example-6 = a sync that keeps what it replaces or deletes
    .example-7 = bring back one file
    .example-8 = compare the source and its copy
    .example-9 = with another configuration file

## The rhel family: Rocky Linux's packages, firewall and SELinux.

man-dnf = installs, removes and searches the packages of a Red Hat family system (Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, RHEL, Fedora): Debian's apt. dnf install PACKAGE… (as root: shows the transaction with its dependencies and asks "Is this ok [y/N]:", -y answers yes), remove PACKAGE…, search WORD (in the names and summaries), info PACKAGE (version, size, repository, description), list installed|available [PATTERN], provides FILE (which package has this file: semanage is in policycoreutils-python-utils; without a slash, dnf also looks in /usr/bin and /usr/sbin), repolist (the repositories: baseos, appstream, extras), history [info N] (each transaction, who and when), makecache, check-update, upgrade. Errors: "No match for argument", "Unable to find a match", "This command has to be run with superuser privileges". Rocky Linux 10 and RHEL 10 ship DNF 4 (dnf5 is Fedora's). Installing a service doesn't start it: systemctl enable --now.
    .synopsis = dnf [-y] install|remove PACKAGE… ; dnf search|info|provides WORD ; dnf list [installed|available] ; dnf repolist ; dnf history [info N]
    .option-y = answer yes to the question (--assumeyes).
    .option-q = quiet: fewer messages.
    .option--assumeyes = the same as -y.
    .option--version = the version.
    .example-1 = the packages whose name or summary says httpd
    .example-2 = a package's version, size and description
    .example-3 = install Apache (and its dependencies)
    .example-4 = which package gives the semanage command
    .example-5 = what is installed
    .example-6 = the repositories
    .example-7 = the transactions so far
man-rpm = queries the RPM database: what is installed, from which package a file comes, whether a package's files changed. -q PACKAGE: its full name (or "package X is not installed", exit 1); -qa: every installed package; -qi: its information; -ql: its files; -qc: its configuration files; -qf FILE: the package that owns it ("is not owned by any package"); -V PACKAGE: verify its files (S size, 5 checksum, T time changed; c a configuration file). Installing and removing go through dnf, which solves the dependencies.
    .synopsis = rpm -q[ailc] PACKAGE… ; rpm -qa ; rpm -qf FILE ; rpm -V PACKAGE
    .option-q = query.
    .option-a = all the installed packages.
    .option-i = the package's information.
    .option-l = the package's files.
    .option-c = only its configuration files.
    .option-f = the package that owns a file.
    .option-V = verify a package's files against the database.
    .option--version = the version.
    .example-1 = is httpd installed, and which version
    .example-2 = every installed package
    .example-3 = httpd's information
    .example-4 = the files of httpd-core
    .example-5 = which package installed this file
    .example-6 = has a package's configuration changed
man-firewall-cmd = drives firewalld, the firewall of Red Hat family systems (Debian's ufw). A network interface belongs to a zone (public by default: cockpit, dhcpv6-client and ssh allowed); a zone allows services (http: 80/tcp, https: 443/tcp…), ports (8010/tcp) and rich rules (rule family="ipv4" source address="198.51.100.0/24" port port="8010" protocol="tcp" accept: from these addresses only). Two configurations: the runtime one, which filters now (firewall-cmd without --permanent), and the permanent one (--permanent, in /etc/firewalld/zones/), which --reload and each start load in place of the runtime one: a change made only at runtime is lost at the next reload or reboot, a change made only with --permanent does nothing until then. Both: the command twice, or --runtime-to-permanent. Changes need root (otherwise "Authorization failed."); they answer "success".
    .synopsis = firewall-cmd [--permanent] [--zone=ZONE] --add-service=NAME|--add-port=PORT/PROTO|--add-rich-rule='RULE' ; firewall-cmd --reload ; firewall-cmd --list-all|--get-active-zones|--state
    .option--state = running or not running.
    .option--reload = load the permanent configuration in place of the runtime one.
    .option--permanent = change (or show) the permanent configuration instead of the runtime one.
    .option--zone = the zone (the default zone otherwise, public).
    .option--get-active-zones = the zones in use and their interfaces.
    .option--get-default-zone = the default zone.
    .option--list-all = everything a zone allows.
    .option--list-services = the zone's services.
    .option--list-ports = the zone's ports.
    .option--list-rich-rules = the zone's rich rules.
    .option--add-service = allow a service (http, https, ssh…).
    .option--remove-service = take a service away.
    .option--add-port = allow a port: 8010/tcp.
    .option--remove-port = take a port away.
    .option--add-rich-rule = add a rich rule (in quotes).
    .option--remove-rich-rule = remove a rich rule.
    .option--runtime-to-permanent = save the runtime configuration as the permanent one.
    .option--query-service = is this service allowed (yes or no).
    .option--info-service = a service's ports.
    .example-1 = is firewalld running
    .example-2 = which zone the interfaces are in
    .example-3 = what the default zone allows (runtime)
    .example-4 = allow HTTP now (lost at the next reload)
    .example-5 = allow HTTP permanently (from the next reload)
    .example-6 = load the permanent configuration now
    .example-7 = port 8010 from the office network only
    .example-8 = keep what was changed at runtime
man-getenforce = prints SELinux's current mode: Enforcing (denials are refused and logged), Permissive (logged only) or Disabled.
    .synopsis = getenforce
    .example-1 = the mode
man-setenforce = switches SELinux between Enforcing (1) and Permissive (0) until the next boot (as root). Permissive refuses nothing: useful a few seconds to confirm that SELinux is the cause, never a fix: the machine loses its protection. The mode at boot is SELINUX= in /etc/selinux/config.
    .synopsis = setenforce Enforcing|Permissive|1|0
    .example-1 = permissive until the next boot (a test, not a fix)
    .example-2 = enforcing again
man-sestatus = SELinux's status: enabled or not, the loaded policy (targeted), the current mode and the one from /etc/selinux/config, the kernel's policy version; -b adds every boolean and its value.
    .synopsis = sestatus [-b]
    .option-b = the booleans and their values.
    .example-1 = the status
    .example-2 = with the booleans
man-getsebool = the value of SELinux booleans: switches the policy offers for common needs (httpd_can_network_connect: httpd may connect to any port; httpd_read_user_content: httpd may read the home directories' files; nis_enabled: every program that uses NSS may bind and connect to unreserved ports…). -a: all of them.
    .synopsis = getsebool -a | getsebool BOOLEAN…
    .option-a = all the booleans.
    .example-1 = all the booleans
    .example-2 = may httpd connect to other ports
man-setsebool = sets SELinux booleans (as root): setsebool NAME on|off. Without -P, until the next boot; with -P, written in the policy too, so it survives a reboot.
    .synopsis = setsebool [-P] BOOLEAN on|off ; setsebool [-P] NAME=VALUE…
    .option-P = permanent: written in the policy, kept at the next boot.
    .example-1 = until the next boot only
    .example-2 = for good
man-chcon = changes a file's SELinux context (user:role:type:level) directly: -t TYPE changes only the type, --reference=FILE copies another's, -R goes into directories. The change is not in the policy: the next relabel (restorecon, an /.autorelabel at boot, fixfiles) puts back what the policy says. To label a place for good: semanage fcontext, then restorecon.
    .synopsis = chcon [-R] [-v] -t TYPE FILE… ; chcon [-R] --reference=REF FILE… ; chcon CONTEXT FILE…
    .option-t = the type to set.
    .option-u = the SELinux user to set.
    .option-r = the role to set.
    .option-R = recursive: the directories and what they hold.
    .option-v = say what is changed.
    .option--reference = take the context of this file.
    .example-1 = a file's context
    .example-2 = a type for one file (until the next relabel)
    .example-3 = like /var/www/html, recursively (until the next relabel)
man-restorecon = puts back the SELinux context the policy gives each path (its file contexts, and the local rules added with semanage fcontext): the type only, unless -F. -R goes into directories, -v says what changes ("Relabeled PATH from OLD to NEW"), -n changes nothing (with -v: what a relabel would do). A file moved with mv keeps the label of where it came from: restorecon fixes it.
    .synopsis = restorecon [-R] [-v] [-n] [-F] PATH…
    .option-R = recursive.
    .option-v = show each change.
    .option-n = change nothing (show what would change, with -v).
    .option-F = force: the whole context, user included.
    .example-1 = what a relabel would change, without changing it
    .example-2 = relabel a site's files from the policy
    .example-3 = one file
man-semanage = manages the SELinux policy's local settings (as root; in policycoreutils-python-utils). fcontext: the labels of paths: -a -t TYPE "SPEC" adds a rule (a regular expression: "/srv/site(/.*)?" for a directory and everything in it), -e /var/www /srv/site makes a path labelled like another, -d removes, -l lists the policy's and the local ones, -l -C only the local ones; the files keep their label until restorecon. port: the type of a port: -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8010 lets the programs allowed on http_port_t (httpd) use it; -l lists them ("already defined, modifying instead" for a port the policy already names). boolean: -l lists the booleans (state, default, description), -m --on|--off NAME sets one for good.
    .synopsis = semanage fcontext -a|-d|-m [-t TYPE] [-f a|f|d] [-e REF] SPEC ; semanage fcontext -l [-C] ; semanage port -a|-d|-m -t TYPE -p tcp|udp PORT ; semanage port -l [-C] ; semanage boolean -l|-m --on|--off NAME
    .option-a = add.
    .option-d = delete.
    .option-m = modify.
    .option-l = list.
    .option-C = with -l, only the local customisations.
    .option-t = the SELinux type.
    .option-p = the protocol (tcp or udp), for port.
    .option-f = the kind of file, for fcontext: a (all), f (regular file), d (directory).
    .option-e = for fcontext: label SPEC like this other path (an equivalence).
    .option-n = without the heading.
    .example-1 = what the policy says of /var/www
    .example-2 = label a directory and its contents like web content, for good
    .example-3 = the same, as an equivalence with /var/www
    .example-4 = the local rules added so far
    .example-5 = the web ports
    .example-6 = let httpd listen on 8010
    .example-7 = the httpd booleans
man-semodule = manages the SELinux policy's modules (as root): -l lists them, -i FILE.pp loads a module (made by audit2allow -M), -r NAME removes one. A module made from the denials allows exactly what was refused, often more than needed: read the rules (the .te file) before loading it.
    .synopsis = semodule -l ; semodule -i FILE.pp ; semodule -r NAME
    .option-l = list the modules.
    .option-i = install a policy package.
    .option-r = remove a module.
    .example-1 = the modules
    .example-2 = load a module
    .example-3 = remove it
man-ausearch = searches the audit log (/var/log/audit/audit.log, as root): -m avc the SELinux denials, -ts recent (the last ten minutes), today, boot or HH:MM since when, -c COMMAND one program's. Each event: ----, its time, its records (PROCTITLE, SYSCALL, AVC). An AVC says who (scontext: httpd_t), what (tcontext: the file's or port's type, tclass), which permission was denied ({"{"} read {"}"}, {"{"} name_bind {"}"}, {"{"} name_connect {"}"}) and whether it was refused (permissive=0). "<no matches>": nothing found.
    .synopsis = ausearch -m avc [-ts recent|today|boot|HH:MM] [-c COMMAND]
    .option-m = the record type (avc).
    .option-ts = since when (recent, today, boot, HH:MM).
    .option-c = one program's events (its comm).
    .option-i = interpret the numbers (accepted, shown raw in the game).
    .example-1 = every denial
    .example-2 = those of the last ten minutes
    .example-3 = httpd's, today
man-audit2why = explains SELinux denials (AVC records read from its input, or the whole log with -a): a boolean that would allow it ("The boolean … was set incorrectly", with the setsebool to type; "One of the following booleans" when several would), or a missing rule ("Missing type enforcement (TE) allow rule"): then the question is usually a wrong label or port type, rarely a new rule. In policycoreutils-python-utils.
    .synopsis = ausearch -m avc | audit2why ; audit2why -a
    .option-a = read the whole audit log.
    .example-1 = why the recent denials happened
    .example-2 = all of them
man-audit2allow = writes the SELinux rules that would allow the denials it reads (-a: the whole log): "allow httpd_t user_home_t:file read;", with a note when a boolean would do. -M NAME writes NAME.te (the rules) and NAME.pp (the module to load with semodule -i). It allows what was refused, not what was meant: a wrong label becomes a permanent hole. Read audit2why first; -w is audit2why. In policycoreutils-python-utils.
    .synopsis = audit2allow [-a] [-w] [-M NAME]
    .option-a = read the whole audit log.
    .option-M = write a module package NAME.pp (and NAME.te).
    .option-w = explain instead (like audit2why).
    .example-1 = the rules the recent denials ask for
    .example-2 = a module from them (read mymodule.te before loading it)
    .example-3 = why, rather than how
